ciated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Data Availability Statement Data is available upon request.',207,'2025-10-03 08:18:12','2025-10-03 08:18:12');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(238,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10297727','Suboptimal Cardiology Follow-Up Among Patients With and Without Cancer Hospitalized for Heart Failure.','Higgason N, Soroka O, Goyal P, Mahmood SS, Pinheiro LC.','2023','kenzik moyo klabunde comorbidy kociol disposing recode dually dually arrythmias cardiotoxic earle kociol wallner nihms1888962 nihms1888962 1k01ca251645 ganiats jessup konstam michl oates rahko yancy e391 paniagua jovani takvorian suthahar splansky januzzi vasan kreger cardiooncol schou gislason køber torp kenzik balentine richman kilgore guha armanious bonsu jneid fradley sheka yancy jessup drazner geraci horwich januzzi masoudi riegel wilkoff barnason deswal kociol wirtz honarpour casebeer kurtz e015042 kociol hammill heidenreich yancy pinder hortobagyi cormier klabunde schrag howlader krapcho kosary ruhl tatalovich mariotto feuer beecy ruffino lachs moyo sileanu cashy radomski donohue hausmann lrm gellad soroka safford klabunde potosky legler pompei ales hammill heidenreich yancy soroka higgason safford cozzolino abraha orso mengoni cerasa eusebi montedori umbria e013785 visaria safford dharmarajan bernheim drye rehospitalization nilo safford derosa hershman accordino buono kalinsky hur earle neville doose demissie bandera litvak batukbhai jeter emens fetting silhy stearns challa calaway deswal kutty vallakati baliga guha reshetnyak levitan safford e006438 reshetnyak akinyemiju safford wallner furgal akc friese nihms1888962 nihms1888962','Abstract Many patients hospitalized for heart failure (HF) do not receive recommended follow-up cardiology care, and Non-White patients are less likely to receive follow-up than Whites. Poor HF management may be particularly problematic among cancer patients because cardiovascular comorbidity can delay cancer treatments. Therefore, we sought to describe outpatient cardiology care patterns in cancer patients hospitalized for HF and to determine if receipt of follow-up varied by race/ethnicity. Surveillance, Epidemiology, and End Results (SEER) data from 2007–2013 linked to Medicare claims from 2006–2014 were used. We included individuals aged 66+ with breast, prostate, or colorectal cancer, and pre-existing HF. Cancer patients were matched to patients in a non-cancer cohort that included individuals with HF and no cancer. The primary outcome was receipt of an outpatient, face-to-face cardiologist visit within 30 days of HF hospitalization. We compared follow-up rates between cancer and non-cancer cohorts, and stratified analyses by race/ethnicity. 2,356 cancer patients and 2,362 non-cancer patients were included. Overall, 43% of cancer patients and 42% of non-cancer patients received cardiologist follow-up (p=0.30). After multivariable adjustment, White patients were 15% more likely to receive cardiology follow-up than Black patients (95% CI: 1.02–1.30). Black cancer patients were 41% (95% CI: 1.11–1.78) and Asian cancer patients were 66% (95% CI: 1.11–2.49) more likely to visit a cardiologist than their non-cancer counterparts. In conclusion, less than half of cancer patients hospitalized for HF received recommended follow-up with a cardiologist, and significant race-related differences in cardiology follow-up exist. Future studies should investigate reasons for these differences. Keywords: Heart failure hospitalization, outpatient follow-up, comorbidity management, racial/ethnic disparities Introduction Heart failure (HF) and cancer represent major causes of hospitalization and death among older adults in the United States. 1 , 2 and frequently co-occur. 3 Nearly 1 in 5 HF patients are also diagnosed with cancer. 4 Prior studies show that patients with HF and cancer experience poorer outcomes than patients with HF alone 5 , 6 and patients with cancer alone. 7 Indeed, patients with cancer hospitalized for HF face a 5.1% risk of inpatient mortality compared to 2.9% among their non-cancer counterparts. 6 The American College of Cardiology and American Heart Association recommend that patients visit a cardiologist within 14 days of hospitalization for HF; 8 , 9 however, only 23% of patients hospitalized for HF receive this follow-up. 10 Using the Surveillance, Epidemiology, and End Results cancer registry linked to Medicare fee-for-service claims (SEER-Medicare), we sought to describe outpatient cardiology care patterns among Medicare beneficiaries hospitalized for HF with incident breast, prostate, or colorectal cancer. We also aimed to determine if cardiology care patterns differed by race/ethnicity, given that Non-White patients without cancer are less likely to receive follow-up care after hospitalization 11 and have increased risk for adverse cardiovascular events compared to White patients. 5 , 12 , 13 Methods This observational matched cohort study used SEER data from 2007–2013 linked to Medicare claims from 2006–2014. The SEER-Medicare dataset includes Medicare fee-for-services beneficiaries aged 65+ years who were diagnosed with cancer in SEER registry regions and includes data on demographics, cancer characteristics, and health services reimbursed by Medicare Parts A and B. 14 SEER-Medicare data encompasses 35% of cancer patients in the United States. 15 It also includes a cohort of Medicare beneficiaries aged 65 years or older residing in a SEER geographic region who do not have cancer. We identified incident cancer cases and matched them to non-cancer patients. We excluded Medicare beneficiaries: 1) diagnosed with cancer from an autopsy or death certificate, 2) with stage 4 cancer, and 3) with a prior history of cancer. This study was deemed exempt by the Weill Cornell Medicine Institutional Review Board. For the cancer cohort, we included Medicare beneficiaries aged 66+ years with incident, non-metastatic breast, prostate, or colorectal cancer, and pre-existing HF at the time of their cancer diagnosis. We identified pre-existing HF using ICD-9 codes 16 in the 365 days before cancer diagnosis ( Supplementary Table 1 ). We followed the approach used by Kenzik and colleagues 5 and operationalized pre-existing HF as at least one inpatient encounter with a diagnosis of HF or two outpatient encounters on separate calendar days with a diagnosis of HF. Given that we wanted to examine outcomes following a hospitalization for HF, we limited our cancer cohort to individuals with a hospitalization. We included index hospitalizations with a primary diagnosis of HF using ICD-9 codes 16 ( Supplementary Table 1 ). Eligible individuals were required to have continuous Part A Medicare coverage from time of cancer diagnosis to index hospitalization (to ensure that the first hospitalization was captured) and two months of continuous Part B Medicare coverage (to ensure cardiologist follow-up was captured). Among the beneficiaries with an index admission for HF, we further excluded individuals who had a length of stay that was 90+ days and who were discharged to a non-home destination, such as a skilled nursing facility or hospice care. Lastly, we excluded individuals who died during index hospitalization and individuals who were readmitted to the hospital within 30 days of index hospitalization ( Figure 1 ). Figure 1. Open in a new tab Exclusion Cascade for Cancer Cohort The cancer cohort included Medicare beneficiaries aged 66+ years with incident, non-metastatic breast, prostate, or colorectal cancer and pre-existing HF before cancer diagnosis. Eligible individuals had continuous Medicare coverage from time of cancer diagnosis to two months following index hospitalization and were not enrolled in an HMO during this period or in the year before cancer diagnosis. Patients who did not have an index HF hospitalization, had a length of stay of 90+ days, died or were readmitted to the hospital during the 30-day follow-up period, or were discharged to a non-home destination such as a skilled nursing facility, were excluded. For the non-cancer cohort, we included Medicare beneficiaries aged 66+ years residing in a SEER geographic region who had HF but no cancer diagnosis. We utilized the same method to identify HF diagnosis as used in the cancer cohort. The same requirements for Medicare coverage and the same exclusion criteria used in the cancer cohort were applied to the non-cancer cohort ( Figure 2 ). Figure 2. Open in a new tab Exclusion Cascade for Non-Cancer Cohort The non-cancer cohort included Medicare beneficiaries aged 66+ with HF and no cancer. Eligible individuals had continuous Medicare coverage from time of cancer pseudo-diagnosis to two months following index hospitalization and were not enrolled in an HMO during this period or in the year before cancer pseudo-diagnosis. We applied the same exclusion criteria to the non-cancer cohort as used in the cancer cohort. Using an approach created by Moyo et al, 17 we matched cancer patients to non-cancer patients by age, sex, and number of comorbid conditions, and assigned cancer patients’ diagnosis dates to their matched non-cancer patients. 17 , 18 We captured comorbid conditions in the 365 days before cancer diagnosis for cancer patients and in the 365 days before pseudo-diagnosis for non-cancer patients using Klabunde’s version 19 of the Charlson Comorbidy Index. 20 Because SEER includes only month and year of diagnosis, we set day of cancer diagnosis to the 15 th . 18 The primary study outcome was an outpatient visit with a cardiologist within 30 days of discharge for a HF hospitalization. We adopted the approach used by Kociol et al 11 and Hernandez et al 21 and defined physician follow-up as a face-to-face outpatient evaluation and management visit after index hospitalization for HF (Healthcare Procedure Coding System codes 992.xx-994.xx, excluding Emergency Department codes). Secondary outcomes included: 1) an outpatient visit with a cardiologist within 7-days and 14-days of HF hospitalization, separately; 2) an outpatient visit with a primary care provider (PCP) within 7-days, 14-days, and 30-days of HF hospitalization, separately; and 3) an outpatient visit with an oncologist within 7-days, 14-days, and 30-days of HF hospitalization, separately. Physician specialty was determined using CMS provider specialty codes ( Supplementary Table 2 ). Informed by Andersen’s Behavioral Model of Health Services Use 22 and consistent with prior work done by Pinheiro and colleagues 23 , we considered the following potential confounders: 1) pre-disposing characteristics (race/ethnicity), 2) enabling factors (census-level income quartile, Medicaid eligibility, SEER geographic region, and urban/rural residence, and 3) evaluated need (cardiac comorbid conditions, Charlson comorbidity). Race/ethnicity was determined using SEER race recode. Individuals were classified as 1) Non-Hispanic White, 2) Non-Hispanic Black, 3) Hispanic, 4) Asian, and 5) Other. Cardiac comorbid conditions in the year before cancer diagnosis or pseudo-diagnosis were determined using validated ICD-9 codes for atrial arrhythmia, ventricular arrhythmia, and coronary artery disease 24 , 25 ( Supplementary Table 1 ). These cardiac comorbidities were chosen because they are common causes of hospital readmission after a hospitalization for HF. 26 We used the same Medicare claims data sources described above to determine status of diseases included in the Charlson index, considering a condition present if the diagnostic code occurred on at least two occasions for outpatient encounters or one occasion for inpatient encounters. 5 Differences in cohort characteristics between cancer cases and matched non-cancer cases were compared using chi-square tests and t-tests. We calculated unadjusted rates of cardiology visits after index HF hospitalization by cancer status and by race/ethnicity. Given the prevalence of our outcome, we then used modified Poisson regression with robust error variance 27 to estimate the adjusted association between cancer status and the likelihood of having outpatient follow-up with a cardiologist within 30 days of discharge from a HF hospitalization. The multivariable-adjusted models included race, census-level income quartile, Medicaid eligibility, SEER geographic region, urban/rural status, and cardiac comorbid conditions. To test the hypothesis that differences in cardiology follow-up between cancer and non-cancer patients may vary across racial/ethnic groups, we examined interactions between presence/absence of cancer and race in our unadjusted and adjusted models. For statistically significant interactions (p<0.05), we ran race-stratified models. We performed analyses using SAS statistical software, version 9.4 (SAS Institute, Cary, NC) and Stata, version 14.2 (StataCorp, College Station, TX). A P value of <0.05 was considered statistically significant. Several secondary analyses were also performed. We examined outpatient follow-up with a cardiologist within 7 days and 14 days of discharge for a hospitalization for HF, separately. Outpatient follow-up with a PCP within 7, 14, and 30 days of a hospitalization for HF were also examined, separately. Finally, we examined outpatient follow-up with an oncologist within 7, 14, and 30 days of HF hospitalization, separately. Results We included 2,356 individuals diagnosed with cancer who had pre-existing HF ( Table 1 ). The average age at incident cancer diagnosis was 79.5 years (SD 7.3 years). 39% had colorectal cancer, 31% had prostate cancer, and 30% had breast cancer. 79% of the cancer patients were Non-Hispanic White, 15% were Non-Hispanic Black, 2% were Hispanic and 2% were Asian. Nearly half (49%) of patients were female. 19% were dually eligible for Medicaid (denoting low socioeconomic status), and 11% resided in a rural area. Two-thirds (66%) had a Charlson comorbidity index greater than 2 (reflecting a greater comorbidity burden). Nearly all (84%) had a history of cardiac comorbidities (defined as having one or more of the following conditions: coronary artery disease, atrial arrhythmias, and ventricular arrhythmias). Table 1. Cohort Characteristics for Cancer and Non-Cancer Patients Non-Cancer Cancer No. 2362 No. 2356 p-value Age at diagnosis, y 0.96 66–69 274 (12%) 277 (12%) 70–74 441 (19%) 449 (19%) 75–79 501 (21%) 503 (21%) 80–84 553 (23%) 559 (24%) 85+ 593 (25%) 568 (24%) Female sex 1171 (50%) 1152 (49%) 0.64 Race/ethnicity <0.001 Non-Hispanic White 1877 (79%) 1864 (79%) Non-Hispanic Black 279 (12%) 357 (15%) Asian 71 (3%) 54 (2%) Hispanic 78 (3%) 43 (2%) Other 57 (2%) 38 (2%) Residence in a rural region 294 (12%) 266 (11%) 0.22 SEER geographic region 0.039 West 821 (35%) 808 (34%) South 729 (31%) 679 (29%) Northeast 493 (21%) 570 (24%) Midwest 319 (14%) 299 (13%) Income quartile 0.004 1 550 (24%) 614 (26%) 2 611 (26%) 554 (24%) 3 611 (26%) 555 (24%) 4 545 (24%) 619 (26%) Medicaid eligibility 516 (22%) 458 (19%) 0.041 Charlson comorbidity score 1.00 0–1 801 (34%) 798 (34%) 2–4 1137 (48%) 1133 (48%) 5+ 424 (18%) 425 (18%) Cardiac comorbidities a 2005 (85%) 1989 (84%) 0.66 Open in a new tab There were 2,362 adults with HF who did not have cancer included in our study ( Table 1 ). Most (79%) were Non-Hispanic White, 12% were Non-Hispanic Black, 3% were Asian, and 3% were Hispanic. Half (50%) were female, 22% were dually eligible for Medicaid, and 12% resided in a rural area. Two-thirds (66%) had a Charlson comorbidity index greater than 2. Nearly all (85%) had a history of cardiac comorbidities (defined as having one or more of the following conditions: coronary artery disease, atrial arrhythmias, and ventricular arrythmias). We found that 43% of cancer patients and 42% of patients with no cancer had an outpatient visit with a cardiologist within 30 days of their index hospitalization for HF (p=0.30). Overall, Non-Hispanic Whites had significantly higher rates of follow-up cardiologist care (44%) compared to Non-Hispanic Blacks (33%, p<0.0001.) The observed difference between Non-Hispanic Whites and Non-Hispanic Blacks persisted after adjustment for income quartile, Medicaid eligibility, SEER geographic region, urban/rural status, and cardiac comorbid conditions. Non-Hispanic White patients were 15% more likely to receive follow-up care from a cardiologist than Non-Hispanic Black patients (aRR 1.15, 95% CI: 1.02–1.30, p=0.03). 46% of all male individuals hospitalized for HF received follow-up within 30 days of hospitalization, and 39% of all female individuals received 30-day follow-up (p<0.0001). The interaction term between cancer status and race/ethnicity was statistically significant (p=0.005). As such, we present race-stratified results for outpatient follow-up with a cardiologist within 30 days of HF hospitalization. Among Non-Hispanic Blacks, 38% of cancer patients and 27% of non-cancer patients had a cardiologist visit (p=0.005). Among Asians, 57% of cancer patients and 35% of non-cancer patients received cardiologist follow-up (p=0.01). Among Non-Hispanic Whites, 44% of cancer patients and 44% of non-cancer patients received a cardiologist visit (p=0.86). Among Hispanics, 37% of cancer patients and 41% of non-cancer patients visited a cardiologist (p=0.68) ( Figure 3 ). Figure 3. Open in a new tab Proportion of Cancer and Non-Cancer Patients Who Received Cardiologist Follow-Up Within 30 Days, by Race Here we present race-stratified results for outpatient follow-up with a cardiologist within 30 days of hospitalization for HF. After multivariable adjustment, Non-Hispanic Black cancer patients were 41% more likely to visit a cardiologist than Non-Hispanic Black patients without cancer (95% CI 1.13–1.81). Similarly, Asian cancer patients were 66% more likely to receive cardiologist follow-up than Asian non-cancer patients (95% CI 1.03–2.38). No statistically significant differences between the cancer and non-cancer cohorts were observed in Whites or Hispanics. After adjustment for census-level income quartile, Medicaid eligibility, SEER geographic region, urban/rural status, and cardiac comorbid conditions, the observed differences between the cancer and non-cancer cohorts among Non-Hispanic Black and Asian sub-groups persisted. Non-Hispanic Black cancer patients were 41% more likely to receive follow-up care from a cardiologist than Non-Hispanic Black patients without cancer (aRR 1.41 95% CI: 1.11–1.78). Similarly, Asian cancer patients were 66% more likely to receive follow-up care than their counterparts without cancer (aRR 1.66; 95% CI 1.11–2.49). In our secondary analysis, we examined rates of follow-up with a cardiologist within 7 days and within 14 days of discharge from a hospitalization for HF. Within 7 days, 13% of cancer patients and 13 % of non-cancer patients received at least one follow-up outpatient visit with a cardiologist (p=0.61). Within 14 days, 27% of cancer patients and 27% of non-cancer patients received at least one follow-up outpatient cardiologist visit (p=0.68). We also considered rates of follow-up with 1) a PCP and 2) an oncologist within 7, 14, and 30 days of hospitalization for HF. Within 7 days of HF hospitalization, 27% of cancer patients and 25% of non-cancer patients received at least one follow-up visit with a PCP (p=0.25). Within 14 days, 45% of cancer patients and 42% of non-cancer patients visited a PCP (p=0.01). Within 30 days, 60% of cancer patients and 57% of non-cancer patients had a PCP visit (p=0.049). Within 7 days of discharge for a HF hospitalization, 3.6% of cancer patients visited an oncologist. Within 14 days, 8.2% of cancer patients had an oncologist visit. Within 30 days, 14% of cancer patients saw an oncologist. Discussion There were three important findings from this study of Medicare beneficiaries with cancer and HF. First, less than half of cancer patients visited a cardiologist within 30 days of HF hospitalization, a finding that reflects a missed opportunity to provide recommended cardiology care to a particularly vulnerable population. Second, Non-Hispanic Blacks with concurrent cancer and HF had lower rates of cardiologist follow-up within 30 days of HF hospitalization compared to Non-Hispanic Whites with cancer and HF, indicating that important race-related differences may exist. Finally, concurrent cancer was associated with increased odds of seeing a cardiologist after HF hospitalization among Non-Hispanic Black and Asian patients, a finding that suggests suboptimal healthcare access or oncologist-directed care in these groups. While cardiology follow-up rates among cancer patients were similar to rates among patients without cancer, the 43% follow-up rate among patients with cancer and HF is cause for concern. Current guidelines suggest that patients hospitalized for HF have a follow-up visit with a cardiologist within 14 days of hospital discharge, 8 , 9 and prior work has shown that receipt of follow-up during this discharge period decreases rates of hospital readmission and mortality. 10 , 28 HF management is critical among adults with concurrent cancer because a worsening cardiomyopathy may limit use of certain cardiotoxic life-saving cancer therapies (anthracyclines, targeted therapies) due to concern for introducing a competing risk of death from worsening HF. 7 In addition, cardiologist involvement may be particularly important in the cancer population because prior studies have shown that comorbidity management often declines as patients undergo cancer treatments. 18 , 29 – 32 For example, Pinheiro et al 18 found that patients with cancer and diabetes had significant declines in diabetes care use, including hemoglobin A1c testing and low-density lipoprotein testing, in the year following cancer diagnosis compared to the year prior. In addition, Earle et al 31 found that 5-year survivors of colorectal carcinoma were less likely than controls without cancer to receive appropriate follow-up care for HF, recommended diabetes care, and preventive services. In this study, Non-Hispanic Black patients with cancer and HF were 26% less likely to receive cardiology follow-up after HF hospitalization compared to Non-Hispanic White patients with cancer and HF. This difference by race is concerning because prior work has shown that Black cancer patients have a higher risk (up to 3-fold) of cancer treatment-related cardiotoxicity compared to their White counterparts. 5 , 12 , 13 , 33 , 34 Racial disparities in cardiology follow-up among adults hospitalized for HF have been described. Kociol et al 11 found that the odds of early cardiology follow-up after HF hospitalization were 16% lower in Black patients compared to patients of other races. However, this is the first study to our knowledge to describe racial differences in follow-up cardiology care among adults hospitalized for HF with concurrent cancer. Prior work has shown that social determinants of health (SDOH), such as educational attainment, income, residence in a health professional shortage area, and public health infrastructure, may have an important cumulative effect on HF hospitalization 35 and cancer mortality. 36 Whether SDOH is driving the observations in this study is unknown and merits further investigation. Interestingly, we found that Non-Hispanic Black and Asian cancer patients had significantly higher rates of cardiology follow-up after HF hospitalization compared to their counterparts with HF alone. To our knowledge, ours is the first study to examine cardiology follow-up after HF hospitalization in these two racial groups. There are multiple possible mechanisms underlying our observation. First, it is possible that cancer treatment connects Black and Asian individuals, who otherwise would have lacked cardiology care access, to the medical system. Another possibility is that many Black and Asian cancer patients receive primary care services, such as referral to cardiology and HF management, from their oncologist. Prior work by Wallner et al 37 found that among survivors of early-stage breast cancer, Asian patients and Black patients were more likely to prefer oncologist-directed preventive care and comorbidity care after cancer diagnosis compared to White patients. Further research is needed to understand why Non-Hispanic Black and Asian patients with cancer and HF receive more recommended cardiology care following a HF hospitalization compared to their counterparts with HF alone. There were several significant limitations in our study. Our study was limited to individuals with incident stage I-III breast, prostate, or colorectal cancer, and the average age at incident cancer diagnosis was 79.5 years. Thus, our results may not be generalizable to younger cancer survivors, individuals with other types of cancer, or patients with metastatic cancer. Additionally, this study uses SEER data from 2007–2013 linked to Medicare claims from 2006–2014. It is possible that practice patterns have changed since then. Our study could not examine unmeasured confounders of racial differences such as structural racism and provider mistrust. Finally, physician follow-up within 30 days of HF hospitalization was used as a surrogate for overall HF care in this study. Conclusions Less than half of patients with incident breast, prostate, or colorectal cancer who were hospitalized for HF had a follow-up visit with a cardiologist within 30 days of hospital discharge, representing a missed opportunity to provide appropriate cardiology care to a particularly vulnerable group of patients. Non-Hispanic Black cancer patients received less follow-up cardiology care after HF hospitalization compared to Non-Hispanic Whites. Among Non-Hispanic Blacks and Asians, cancer patients were more likely to receive recommended cardiology care than patients without cancer. Further studies should investigate underlying causes for these race-related differences. Supplementary Material 1 NIHMS1888962-supplement-1.pptx (35.1KB, pptx) 2 NIHMS1888962-supplement-2.pptx (35.4KB, pptx) Funding: National Cancer Institute 1K01CA251645 Footnotes Declaration of interests The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper. Disclosures : Authors have no conflicts of interest to disclose. Publisher''s Disclaimer: This is a PDF file of an unedited manuscript that has been accepted for publication. As a service to our customers we are providing this early version of the manuscript. The manuscript will undergo copyediting, typesetting, and review of the resulting proof before it is published in its final form. Please note that during the production process errors may be discovered which could affect the content, and all legal disclaimers that apply to the journal pertain. References 1. Hunt SA, Abraham WT, Chin MH, Feldman AM, Francis GS, Ganiats TG, Jessup M, Konstam MA, Mancini DM, Michl K, Oates JA, Rahko PS, Silver MA, Stevenson LW, Yancy CW. 2009 focused update incorporated into the ACC/AHA 2005 Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Management of Heart Failure in Adults: a report of the American College of Cardiology Foundation/American Heart Association Task Force on Practice Guidelines: developed in collaboration with the International Society for Heart and Lung Transplantation. Circulation 2009;119:e391–479. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Siegel RL, Miller KD, Jemal A. Cancer statistics, 2020. CA Cancer J Clin 2020;70:7–30. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Lau ES, Paniagua SM, Liu E, Jovani M, Li SX, Takvorian K, Suthahar N, Cheng S, Splansky GL, Januzzi JL Jr., Wang TJ, Vasan RS, Kreger B, Larson MG, Levy D, de Boer RA, Ho JE. Cardiovascular Risk Factors are Associated with Future Cancer. JACC CardioOncol 2021;3:48–58. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Schwartz B, Schou M, Gislason GH, Køber L, Torp-Pedersen C, Andersson C. Prevalence and incidence of various Cancer subtypes in patients with heart failure vs matched controls. Int J Cardiol 2020;316:209–213. [ D