on event driven trials (n=18,809) were primarily treated for prostate (35%), head & neck (20%), and brain (19%) cancers. The median age of patients was 63 years (range 18–91). Patients were primarily male (70%), had private insurance (27%), Zubrod performance status of 0 at study entry (61%), were Caucasian and not Hispanic (68%), and were married or had a live-in partner (46%). Again, some patients did not report education completed (36%) or employment status (48%). Of the remainder, 34% had attended at least some college and 49% were retired. Patients were primarily treated at X full member institutions (42%) and were treated in the US Midwest (27%) or US South (24%) [ Table 1 ]. The median follow-up time for event driven trials was 37.2 months (range 0.1 – 172.4) [ Table 2 ], where most patients were evaluated for an overall survival endpoint (71%) [ Supplemental Table 1 ]. The 1- and 5-year attrition rates were 0.5% (95% CI: 0.4%, 0.6%) and 13.6% (95% CI: 13.1%, 14.1%) [ Table 2 , Figure 1 ]. Multiple Regression Models Variables excluded due to large amounts of missing data overall were marital status (30% missing), education completed (34% missing), employment status (61% missing), and insurance (34% missing). Significant trial-related covariates for decreased hazard of attrition in the complete case analysis were recently activated trials (HR=0.44, 95% CI: 0.40, 0.48), disease site, specifically brain/CNS and bladder/prostate cancers, and those with an event driven endpoint (HR=0.54, 95% CI: 0.48–0.61) [ Table 3 ]. Full member sites (HR=1.10, 95% CI: 1.03–1.18) and sites located in the US New England/Midwest (vs. international sites HR=1.13, 95% CI: 1.02–1.25) were associated with decreased attrition. Patient-level characteristics that were significantly associated with decreased attrition were Zubrod performance status 0 (HR=3.66, 95% CI: 3.33, 4.03), and male gender (HR=0.84, 95% CI: 0.77, 0.92). Incorporation of marital status and education into this model had similar results with the addition of marital status being significantly associated with attrition (HR=1.12, 95% CI: 1.04, 1.22) but not education [ Supplemental Table 3 ]. Table 3. Time to Attrition Cause-Specific Cox Proportional Hazards Multiple Regression Model Complete Case Variables Hazard Ratio 95%CI P-value Age ( ≥65 vs. <65 [RL]) 0.876 (0.81–0.95) 0.0007 Year of Trial Activation (≥1994 vs. <1994 [RL]) 0.440 (0.40–0.48) <.0001 Institution Type (CCOP/ Affiliate/Satellite/Not an RTOG vs. Full Member [RL]) 1.102 (1.03–1.18) 0.0054 Institution Region (US New England /Midwest [RL] vs. US South/West 1.069 (0.99–1.15) 0.0595 International) 1.126 (1.02–1.25) 0.0249 Zubrod Performance Status ( ≥1 vs. <1 [RL]) 3.662 (3.33–4.03) <.0001 Gender (Male vs. Female[RL]) 0.840 (0.77–0.92) 0.0002 Type of Endpoint (Event Driven vs. Fixed [RL]) 0.542 (0.48–0.61) <.0001 Disease Site (Brain [RL] vs. Gastrointestinal 1.893 (1.63–2.20) <.0001 Head & Neck 1.408 (1.22–1.63) <.0001 Sarcoma & Lung 3.568 (3.03–4.21) <.0001 Bladder & Prostate 0.919 (0.79–1.08) 0.2917 Gynecological & Breast 1.214 (1.03–1.44) 0.0238 Patients Lost to Attrition/Total 3788/22660 Patients Lost to Death 8221 Open in a new tab RL = Reference Level Cause-specific hazard multiple regression models using imputed data showed similar results [ Table 4 ]. Recently activated trials (HR=0.43, 95% CI: 0.40, 0.47), certain disease sites, specifically bladder/prostate cancer (HR=0.75, 95% CI: 0.65–0.86), and those with an event driven endpoint (HR=0.56, 95% CI: 0.51, 0.62) were associated with a decreased attrition. Full member sites were associated with decreased attrition (HR=1.16, 95% CI: 1.09–1.23) and institution region where patients from institutions in the US South/West had an increased attrition compared to US New England/Midwest (HR=1.12, 95% CI: 1.05, 1.19). Patient-level characteristics that were significantly associated with decreased attrition were Zubrod performance status 0 (HR=2.99, 95% CI: 2.75, 3.25) and male gender (HR=0.90, 95% CI: 0.82, 0.98). Table 4. Time to Attrition Cause-Specific Cox Proportional Hazards Multiple Regression Model Imputed Analysis Variables Hazard Ratio 95%CI P-value Year of Trial Activation (≥1994 vs. <1994 [RL]) 0.434 (0.40–0.47) <.0001 Institution Type (CCOP/ Affiliate/Satellite/Not an RTOG vs. Full Member [RL]) 1.160 (1.09–1.23) <.0001 Institution Region (US New England /Midwest [RL] vs. US South/West 1.118 (1.05–1.19) 0.0005 International) 1.097 (0.99–1.21) 0.0620 Zubrod Performance Status ( ≥1 vs. <1 [RL]) 2.993 (2.75–3.25) <.0001 Gender (Male vs. Female[RL]) 0.898 (0.82–0.98) 0.0136 Type of Endpoint (Event Driven vs. Fixed [RL]) 0.559 (0.51–0.62) <.0001 Disease Site (Brain [RL] vs. Gastrointestinal 1.484 (1.29–1.71) <.0001 Head & Neck 1.019 (0.89–1.16) 0.7846 Sarcoma & Lung 2.543 (2.19–2.95) <.0001 Bladder & Prostate 0.75 (0.65–0.86) <.0001 Gynecological & Breast 0.979 (0.84–1.14) 0.7938 Patients Lost to Attrition/Total 4519/27443 at each iteration of imputation Patients Lost to Death 9700 Open in a new tab RL = Reference Level Discussion This is one of the first studies to expansively focus on attrition in the National Clinical Trials Network’s radiation oncology based clinical trials. As expected, the attrition rates for both fixed and event trials in this secondary data analysis increased significantly over the duration of the trial. Generally, attrition rates were overall higher for fixed-time trials in comparison to event driven trials given the high rates for fixed time trials with follow-up < 6 months. This may be expected for several reasons. First, a quarter of the fixed trials were Phase I (25.4%) and 44% Phase II, whereas the majority of event-driven trials were Phase III (92.5%). Second, most participants in fixed trials had diagnoses of lung, gastrointestinal, brain, prostate, gynecologic, and head and neck cancers, cancers for which the survival prognosis is variable based on the aggressive nature of these types of cancer (e.g., non-small lung cancer). Estimates of attrition over time in these clinical trials as well as the factors associated with increased or decreased rates of attrition are important data that can help in trial design, and in developing strategies for recruitment and retention. Concerns about attrition and trial retention are significant due to the possible impact on study validity and completion. Moreover, patients lost to attrition in fixed trials, for example, may not be included in the primary analysis whereas for event-driven trials, the patient might be censored at their last known follow-up and included in the analysis. Attrition in event-driven trials steadily increased over time as well, with 13.6% attrition at 5 years, 29.9% at 10 years, and 47.5% at 15 years, respectively. However, without comparative data about attrition or details about statistical assumptions and trial specifics, it is difficult to assess the possible impact of this level of attrition on the studies themselves. The attrition rates and death rates raise important questions surrounding the role of palliative care in clinical trials. 11 – 12 Much theoretical work has outlined the therapeutic benefit of palliative care but an article by Temel and colleagues 11 found that early integration of palliative care for patients with metastatic lung cancer significantly improved survival and quality of life; and importantly, less than 1% of their sample dropped out of the study. Brunetto and colleagues 13 reported several patient baseline characteristics (age greater than 60 years, 3 or more metastatic sites, high serum LDH, and albumin <35 g/dL trending to significance) were associated with 21% of unplanned patient hospitalizations in Phase I trials and contributed to an early risk of attrition (6.7%). The majority of unplanned admissions were for disease-related treatments and symptom management while 22% were for management of treatment-related toxicities. XXXX, and colleagues 11 argue that palliative care is often not perceived as compatible with clinical trial participation yet can provide significant benefits for trial participants in terms of pain control, spiritual needs, and overall mental health support. It would be important to document how often XXXX trials include palliative care and symptom management for participants in their clinical trials, and whether and how inclusion of such services affects attrition rates over time. The results from this analysis show that attrition rates vary by disease site, with sarcoma and lung cancers having an increased rate of attrition and brain and bladder and prostate cancers having decreased rates of attrition. With any type of cancer diagnosis, symptom burden is a concern. In fact, Bubis and colleagues reported that cancer site was significantly associated with higher odds of symptom burden, particularly for those with respiratory and oropharyngeal cancers. 14 Patients might suffer from a constellation of emotional, cognitive, and physical symptoms that affect their ability to participate in research. A more in-depth analysis as well as additional data is needed to appreciate the specific issues for participants with cancers in different disease sites who were lost to attrition and the types of formal and informal support mechanisms they might need to remain actively enrolled on a trial. Better understanding is needed of the different types of symptoms that might lead to attrition based on disease site and the type of treatment modalities that participants are receiving, including participation in cancer clinical trials. In-depth qualitative data could begin to explore these issues, particularly as they relate to symptom burden and distress. The enrollment of women in research continues to be an issue of concern for all researchers despite establishment of federal guidelines for their inclusion in human subjects research in 1993. 15 A report from the National Institutes of Health (NIH), however, suggests that a larger percentage of women are participating in NIH clinical research and Phase III trials since 1993. 16 Yet, these data do not specify the percentage of women enrolled in cancer clinical trials, which seem to be lagging behind. In our sample, slightly more than a third of participants in fixed trials were women, while approximately 29.5% of participants in event-related trials were women. This is consistent with the findings of Jargsi and colleagues who reported that women remained underrepresented in published clinical cancer research studies; in their work, an average of 38.8% of women participated in non-sex-specific studies and there was a lower proportion of women included in the 7 non-sex specific cancer types that they assessed (e.g., lung, head and neck, gastrointestinal, hematological). 17 To some degree, it is not surprising to find that women are more likely than men to be lost to attrition. Today, many women are financially self-sufficient, raising children on their own, taking care of older parents, and involved in other life/care-giving role activities. A better picture is needed of how women’s’ caregiving role and gender differences affect clinical trial participation and retention and the amount of support that women need once enrolled in a trial. In the cohort that we analyzed, attrition was also higher in X institutions in the West and in the South. Both of these areas in the country generally have higher percentages of minority populations. The number of minority participants in our sample was lower than Caucasians overall, however, we don’t have data on the breakdown by region. For example, Hispanics now comprise about 17% of the U.S. population and more than 50% of the Hispanic population resides in three states, including California, Texas, and Florida. 18 More process oriented data on the experiences of minorities participating in research within these particular XXXX sites is clearly needed. Real-time data capture of minority participants’ day-to-day experiences of participating in clinical trials may provide us with insights on factors influencing retention. The main strength of this study includes the extensive review of XXXX radiation-based cancer trials over an extended period of time. Nonetheless, there are several limitations. First, a measure of toxicity was not included because of the variability in measuring toxicity throughout the years. Future studies need to take this into consideration as symptom-related distress and toxicity could be a significant predictor of attrition. Patients experience various degrees of symptom burden associated with cancer and cope differently depending on their degree of tolerance and what experimental side effects they are willing to accept to extend their life or remain hopeful for a cure. However, the relationship between disease status, symptom burden, toxicity and attrition is not known. As symptoms progress or remain unabated, it could significantly impact retention in a clinical trial and would be important to assess in future work. Because of the retrospective review of our data and the limitations associated with collecting certain demographic variables in earlier time periods, we don’t know, for example, what role social support networks or the support of a loving partner, spouse, or friend might play in retention. When we assessed marital status using a subgroup of patients with complete data, however, we found that those who were married or had a live-in-partner were more likely lost to attrition. Future research should also examine these types of issues. The results suggest several areas for consideration. First, because attrition of women in XXXX cancer clinical trials remains a considerable issue, further dialogue, research, and targeted efforts are needed to understand the specific concerns of diverse groups of women. Additionally, the limited research on gender differences and retention in clinical trials demands more work on life-balance issues that arise during the course of study participation, especially for women. Indeed, in some cases women continue to be underrepresented in clinical trials yet these clinical trials are used to formulate women’s health guidelines. 19 Second, many patients recognize that they have an incurable disease yet still choose to participate in research for a variety of reasons. Integrating palliative care within clinical trials can allow patients to participate in research and simultaneously address their symptoms, psychological or other immediate individual, family, and health-related needs. Innovative models of care are needed that provide a seamless transition from clinical trial participation to quality end-of-life care. Third, for trials with a time-to-event endpoint, (i.e. longer follow-up times), general efforts to keep patients engaged such as scheduling the next visit at the time of the last visit and telephone contact may benefit from additional electronic contact using text and email messages or other types of social media. In conclusion, this data suggest more research is needed to examine the rates of attrition in different kinds of trials, reasons for higher attrition in women, younger individuals, those enrolled in geographically diverse institutions as well as further dialogue on the role of palliative care within clinical trials given the attrition rates for fixed trials. In addition, it would be useful to begin a dialogue among the different cooperative groups on the development of innovative strategies to address retention issues specific to each network group. Supplementary Material 1 NIHMS1508890-supplement-1.docx (22.3KB, docx) Acknowledgments This project was supported by grants U10CA21661, U10CA180868, U10CA180822 from the National Cancer Institute (NCI) and this project is funded in part by the Pennsylvania Department of Health Formula Grant 4100037703. The Department specifically declaims responsibility for any analyses, interpretations or conclusions. Footnotes Publisher''s Disclaimer: This is a PDF file of an unedited manuscript that has been accepted for publication. As a service to our customers we are providing this early version of the manuscript. The manuscript will undergo copyediting, typesetting, and review of the resulting proof before it is published in its final citable form. Please note that during the production process errors may be discovered which could affect the content, and all legal disclaimers that apply to the journal pertain. Conflicts of interest : Nothing to disclose, Dr. Bruner, Dr. Curran, Ms. Deshmukh, Dr. Grady, Dr. Hanlon and Dr. Ulrich. Dr. Pugh reports grants from State of Pennsylvania Department of Health, during the conduct of the study; grants from PCORI and the State of Pennsylvania Department of Health, outside the submitted work. References 1. Murthy VH., Krumholz HM, Gross CP. (2004). Participation in cancer clinical trials: race-, sex-, and age-based disparities. JAMA. 2004; 291(22):2720–2726. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Shavers VL, Lynch CF, Burmeister LF (2002). Racial differences in factors that influence the willingness to participate in medical research studies. Ann Epidemiol. 2002;12(4):248–56. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Adams-Campbell LL, Ahaghotu C, Gaskins M, Dawkins FW, Smoot D, Polk OD, Gooding R, DeWitty RL. Enrollment of African Americans onto clinical treatment trials: study design barriers. J Clin Onc. 2004; 22(4):730–734. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Advani AS, Atkeson B, Brown CL, Peterson BL, Fish L, Johnson JL, Gockerman JP, Gautier M. Barriers to the participation of African-American patients with cancer in clinical trials: A pilot study. Cancer. 2003; 97(6):1499–506. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Brown DR, Fouad MN, Basen-Engquist K, Tortolero-Luna G. Recruitment and retention of minority women in cancer screening, prevention, and treatment trials. Ann Epidemiol. 2000; 10(8 Suppl):S13–21. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Ulrich CM,James JL,Walker EM, et al. RTOG physician and research associate attitudes, beliefs and practices regarding clinical trials: implications for improving patient recruitment Contemp Clin Trials, 31 (2010), pp. 221–228 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Gaynor JJ, Feuer EJ, Tan CC, Wu DH, Little CL, Straus SJ, Clarkson BD, Brennan MF. On the use of cause-specific failure and conditional failure probabilities: examples from clinical oncology data. J Amer Statist Assoc 1993;88:400–409 [ Google Scholar ] 8. Cox DR. Regression Models and Life-Tables. J Roy Stat Soc. Series B (Methodological), 1972; 34: 187–220. [ Google Scholar ] 9. Dignam JJ, Zhang Q, Kocherginsky M. The use and interpretation of competing risks regression models. Clin Cancer Res 2012; 18(8): 2301–2308 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Schafer JL. Analysis of Incomplete Multivariate Data, New York: Chapman and Hall; 1997. [ Google Scholar ] 11. Temel JS, Greer JA, Muzikansky A, Gallagher ER, Admane S, Jackson VA, Dahlin CM, Blinderman CD, Jacobsen J, Pirl WF, Billings JA, Lynch TJ. Early palliative care for patients with metastatic non-small-cell lung cancer. N Engl J Med. 2010; 363(8):733–742. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Ulrich C, Grady C, Wendler D, Palliative care: A supportive adjunct to pediatric phase I clinical trials for anticancer agents? Pediatrics, 114 (2003), pp. 852–855 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Brunetto AT, Ang JE, Olmos D, Tan D, Barriuso J, Arkenau HT, et al. A study of the pattern of hospital admissions in a specialist Phase I oncology trials unit: Unplanned admissions as an early indicator of patient attrition. European J Cancer. 2010; 46: 2739–2745. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Bubis LD, Davis L, Mahar A, Barbera A, Li Q, Moody L, et al. Symptom burden in the first year after cancer diagnosis: An analysis of patient-reported outcomes. J Clin Oncol. 2018; 36: Mar 1:JCO2017760876. doi: 10.1200/JCO.2017.76.0876. [Epub ahead of print]; [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. NIH Revitalization Act. 1993. Available at: http://grants.nih.gov/grants/funding/women_min/guidelines_amended_10_2001.htm . Accessed May 4, 2011. 16. Department of Health and Human Services, National Institutes of Health. Monitoring Adherence to the NIH Policy on the Inclusion of Women and Minorities As Subjects in Clinical Research. 2013. Available at: https://orwh.od.nih.gov/resources/pdf/Inclusion-ComprehensiveReport-FY-2011-2012.pdf . Accessed December 3, 2016. 17. Jagsi R, Motomura AR, Amarnath S, Jankovic A, Sheets N, Ubel PA. Under-representation of Women in High-impact Published Clinical Cancer Research. Cancer. 2009; 115(14); 3293–3301. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 18. Hispanics in the U.S. Fast Facts. Retrieved from: http://www.kwch.com/news/national/hispanics-in-the-us-fast-facts/38340348 March 7, 2016. 19. Melloni C, Berger JS, Wang TY, Gunes F, Stebbins A, Pieper KS, et al. Representation of women in randomized clinical trials of cardiovascular disease prevention. Circ Cardiovasc Qual Outcomes. 2010;3:135–142. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials 1 NIHMS1508890-supplement-1.docx (22.3KB, docx)',1040,'2025-10-03 13:23:26','2025-10-03 13:23:26');
