Google Scholar ] 16. Zhao L, Claggett B, Tian L, et al. On the restricted mean survival time curve in survival analysis. Biometrics. 2016;72(1):215-221. doi: 10.1111/biom.12384 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 17. Seruga B, Pond GR, Hertz PC, Amir E, Ocana A, Tannock IF. Comparison of absolute benefits of anticancer therapies determined by snapshot and area methods. Ann Oncol. 2012;23(11):2977-2982. doi: 10.1093/annonc/mds174 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 18. Saltz LB. The value of considering cost, and the cost of not considering value. J Clin Oncol. 2016;34(7):659-660. doi: 10.1200/JCO.2015.64.7867 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 19. Guthrie B, Thompson A, Dumbreck S, et al. Better guidelines for better care: accounting for multimorbidity in clinical guidelines—structured examination of exemplar guidelines and health economic modelling. Health Services and Delivery Research. 2017;5(16):51-71. doi: 10.3310/HSDR05160. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 20. Akobeng AK. Understanding measures of treatment effect in clinical trials. Arch Dis Child. 2005;90(1):54-56. doi: 10.1136/adc.2004.052233 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 21. Royston P, Parmar MKB. An approach to trial design and analysis in the era of non-proportional hazards of the treatment effect. Trials. 2014;15(1):314. doi: 10.1186/1745-6215-15-314 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 22. Royston P, Parmar MKB. The use of restricted mean survival time to estimate the treatment effect in randomized clinical trials when the proportional hazards assumption is in doubt. Stat Med. 2011;30(19):2409-2421. doi: 10.1002/sim.4274 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Supplement. eTable 1. Characteristics of Included Studies eTable 2. Framework Scores and RMST Difference Values eTable 3. Mean RMST Differences (Months) for ESMO-PMCBGs Ranging From 1 to 4 eTable 4. ICC Values eTable 5. Primary Analysis With OS and PFS Data Split eFigure 1. Distribution of ASCO-CBS eFigure 2. Distribution of ESMO-PMCBG eFigure 3. Spearman Correlations of ASCO Clinical Benefit Score (ASCO-CBS) and ESMO Preliminary Magnitude of Clinical Benefit Grade (ESMO-PMCBG) vs Median Survival Difference Click here for additional data file. (526.4KB, pdf)',254,'2025-10-03 13:23:53','2025-10-03 13:23:53');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(258,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9677193','American Society for Radiation Oncology''s Advances in Radiation Oncology in 2022.','Miller RC, Tsai CJ.','2022','astro citescore citescore astro cybersecurity cyberattacks ugurluer flavin cyberattack abscopal okhmatdyt rtts heroes bohdana bachynska yana kuts bohdana okhmatdyt wounded yana reirradiation fattahi influencers astro diligence ugurluer ozyar corapcioglu hostilities flavin cyberattack gelblum lehrer abscopal fattahi reirradiation influencers kovalchuk zelinskyi hanych bombs','This fall marks the American Society for Radiation Oncology''s (ASTRO) Advances in Radiation Oncology ’s seventh year of publishing. The past year has been our most successful to date with our CiteScore increasing to 4.3 ( Fig. 1 ), placing Advances in Radiation Oncology in the top 3 of comparable journals. A conventional impact factor is expected to be granted in 2023. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Advances in Radiation Oncology CiteScore value and percentage ranking among peer journals. ASTRO created Advances in Radiation Oncology in 2015 in response to the increasing demand for outlets to publish quality radiation oncology–related research and limitations in the number of manuscripts that could be published in conventional paper journals. As a gold open-access journal, permanently freely available, Advances in Radiation Oncology is available on a global basis to all health care professionals and scientists, as well as our patient population and their caregivers. Figure 2 demonstrates Advances in Radiation Oncology ’s complementary relationship with the Red Journal and Practical Radiation Oncology. Figure 3 highlights our current most-downloaded articles. Figure 2. Open in a new tab Scope of American Society for Radiation Oncology journals. Figure 3. Open in a new tab Top downloads in Advances in Radiation Oncology in August 2022. We are particularly interested this year in publishing more scholarly critical reviews. Our current call for articles includes those relating to the use of radiopharmaceuticals and cybersecurity issues in radiation oncology. In recent years, the disruption in care delivery from issues (eg, COVID-19; cyberattacks; and ongoing humanitarian crises in Ukraine [ Fig. 4 ], Syria, and elsewhere) have been the focus of many of our most-read articles. Two articles are included in this collection. A report from Istanbul by Uğurluer et al 1 describes the psychosocial issues facing Ukrainian families whose children were receiving cancer care in Turkey at the onset of the war in Ukraine. Flavin et al 2 report on the national effect on radiation therapy delivery after a cyberattack on the public health services of the Republic of Ireland. Additional review articles included in this issue are a review of the management of implantable devices by Chan et al 3 and on the Abscopal effect by Hatten et al. 4 Figure 4. Open in a new tab OKHMATDYT''s RTTs heroes: Bohdana Bachynska (left) and Yana Kuts (right). Bohdana stayed in OKHMATDYT 24/7 to scan wounded patients, and Yana serves in the Ukrainian army as an emergency medical technician. 7 We welcome inquiries regarding proposed review articles and encourage potential authors to contact the editor in chief directly at miller.robert@mayo.edu to discuss their proposal. Our most downloaded article at this time is a paper on breast cancer reirradiation by Fattahi et al. 5 Social media–focused articles and analyses of professional issues related to resident training and the job market are also highly read. Included in this issue is a report characterizing Twitter influencers in radiation oncology by Valle et al 6 that is one of our most highly read articles. We thank the Advances in Radiation Oncology editorial board, our reviewers, and the ASTRO community for all their time and diligence dedicated to our past 7 years of success. We look forward to continued growth. Advances in Radiation Oncology is committed to diversity and inclusion on the editorial team. Footnotes Sources of support: This work had no specific funding. Disclosures: Dr Miller and Dr Tsai report income from the American Society for Radiation Oncology. Data sharing statement: All data comes from freely available public Internet sites and is referenced. References 1. Uğurluer G, Özyar E, Corapcioglu F, Miller RC. Psychosocial impact of the war in Ukraine on pediatric cancer patients and their families receiving oncological care outside their country at the onset of hostilities. Adv Radiat Oncol. 2002;7 doi: 10.1016/j.adro.2022.100957. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Flavin A, O''Toole E, Murphy L, et al. A national cyberattack affecting radiation therapy: The Irish experience. Adv Radiat Oncol. 2022;7 doi: 10.1016/j.adro.2022.100914. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Chan MF, Young C, Gelblum D, et al. A review and analysis of managing commonly seen implanted devices for patients undergoing radiation therapy. Adv Radiat Oncol. 2021;6 doi: 10.1016/j.adro.2021.100732. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Hatten SJ, Jr, Lehrer EJ, Liao J, et al. A patient-level data meta-analysis of the Abscopal effect. Adv Radiat Oncol. 2022;7 doi: 10.1016/j.adro.2022.100909. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Fattahi S, Ahmed SK, Park SS, et al. Reirradiation for locoregional recurrent breast cancer. Adv Radiat Oncol. 2020;6 doi: 10.1016/j.adro.2020.100640. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Valle LF, Chu FI, Smith M, et al. Characterizing Twitter influencers in radiation oncology. Adv Radiat Oncol. 2022;7 doi: 10.1016/j.adro.2022.100919. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Kovalchuk N, Zelinskyi R, Hanych A, et al. Radiation therapy under the falling bombs: A tale of 2 Ukrainian cancer centers. Adv Radiat Oncol. 2022;7 doi: 10.1016/j.adro.2022.101027. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',5,'2025-10-03 13:24:08','2025-10-03 13:24:08');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(259,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11162903','Impact of Apolipoprotein E Genotype on Neurocognitive Function in Patients With Brain Metastases: An Analysis of NRG Oncology''s RTOG 0614.','Wefel JS, Deshmukh S, Brown PD, Grosshans DR, Sulman EP, Cerhan JH, Mehta MP, Khuntia D, Shi W, Mishra MV, Suh JH, Laack NN, Chen Y, Curtis AA, Laba JM, Elsayed A, Thakrar A, Pugh SL, Bruner DW.','2023','hvlt excitotoxicity mineralizing excitotoxicity noncompetitive excitotoxic excitotoxicity heterozygote homozygote karnofsky given14 rpa ctb hvlt cowa jsw ctb ctb hvlt cowa calero 500ml buffy nuclease rs429358 112c gctgggcgcggacatggaggacgtg gcggccgcctggtgcagtaccgcgg rs7412 158c ccgcgatgccgatgacctgcagaag gcctggcagtgtaccaggccggggc hybridize fluorogenic carboxyfluorescein vic microtiter vic viia7 hvlt recog cowa ctb imputations rci ged rpa hvlt recog ctb hvlt hvlt hvlt cowa ctb hvlt hvlt hvlt cowa cowa ctb hvlt cowa ctb hvlt hvlt hvlt tmtb ctb hvlt tmtb ctb ctb contouring ncctg n107c huntoon huntoon huntoon affording huntoon huntoon ncctg n107c excitotoxicity relatedly coalesce cc001 affording nihms1980152 u10ca180868 u10ca180822 sdmc ug1ca189867 ncorp bruner cerhan elsayed grosshans laack khuntia karyopharm sapience zap mevion xoft tocagen chimerix oncoceutics novocure brainlab varian zai novocure novocure novocure novocure neutron theraputics sulman novocure novocure zai thakrar 5ug1ca190000 stroger shcc ncorp 5ug1ca190000 wefel juno novocure vanquish nct00566852 nctn ncorp nctn khuntia pirzkall debus lohr fuss rhein engenhart cabillic aoyama tago shirato jrosg sperduto mesko cagney aizer nesbit kruser braunstein sahgal shanley sperduto boggs masucci roberge remick plichta buatti gaspar tjc chuong aoyama jaeckle ballman farace cerhan orrego transmitter danysz karcz kubicha glycineb excitotoxicity monje monje mizumatsu fike goodus azzam levison souayah perturbs subventricular eom mglur1 c17 e0147538 aubert sonntag mitschelen duman tzeng bai1 synaptogenesis par3 tiam1 parihar limoli corniola chakraborti rosi fike e40844 tozzi nitropropionic tariot farlow grossberg gergel mobius stoffler mmm mmm500 orgogozo rigaud stoffler mobius forette mmm laack wefel khuntia not114 wefel noll scheurer e97 e108 mahley weisgraber s183 jlr r800069 jlr200 durakoglugil xian reinvang espeseth westlye neubiorev rawle bendayan kuh s41398 caselli dueck sabbagh ahern epsilon4 genin hannequin wallon belloy napolioni greicius gandy dekosky gridiron 134ed4 wagle farner flekkoy epsilon4 koleck sereika e313 e325 ahles satagopan baser betensky associtation e2414 e2428 benedict rhb schretlen groninger tombaugh ruff benton calero hortiguela bullido calero jneumeth truax gutman e0152901 cacciaglia epsilon4 dowell prowse tabet rusted terashima burggren ercoli bookheimer interconnectivity fnagi satagopan kryza lacombe dimitriadoy lyo deangelis orlow noz094 wefel sulman boehling tsavachidis etzel kahalley scheurer bondy huntoon ballman twohy doolet noac262 bouras 0b013e31825018f7 carlin royston kenward b2393 b2393 jch e27 e638 nihms1980152 nctn ncorp nctn','Abstract Purpose: Whole brain radiotherapy (WBRT) is a common treatment for brain metastases (BM) and is frequently associated with decline in neurocognitive functioning (NCF). The e4 allele of the apolipoprotein E (APOE) gene is associated with increased risk of Alzheimer’s disease, and NCF decline associated with a variety of neurologic diseases and insults. APOE carrier status has not been evaluated as a risk factor for onset-time or extent of NCF impairment in patients with BM treated with WBRT. Methods and Materials: NRG/RTOG 0614 treated adult patients with brain metastases with 37.5 Gy of WBRT (+/− memantine), performed longitudinal NCF testing, and included an optional blood draw for APOE analysis. NCF test results were compared at baseline and over time with mixed effects models. A cause-specific Cox model for time to NCF failure was performed to assess the effects of treatment arm and APOE carrier status. Results: APOE results were available for 45% (n=227/508) of patients. NCF did not differ by APOE e4 carrier status at baseline. Mixed effects modeling showed that APOE e4 carriers had worse memory after WBRT compared to APOE e4 non-carriers (HVLT-R Total Recall [least square (LS) mean difference = 0.63, p=0.0074], Delayed Recognition [LS mean difference=0.75, p=0.023]). However, APOE e4 carrier status was not associated with time to NCF failure (HR=0.86, 95% CI: 0.60–1.23, p=0.40). Memantine delayed the time to NCF failure, regardless of carrier status (HR=0.72, 95% CI: 0.52–1.01, p=0.054). Conclusions: APOE e4 carriers with brain metastases exhibited greater decline in learning and memory, executive function, and the Clinical Trial Battery Composite score after treatment with WBRT (+/− memantine), without acceleration of onset of difference in time to NCF failure. Keywords: brain metastasis, neurocognitive function, quality of life, apolipoprotein e INTRODUCTION Brain metastases are the most common intracranial malignancy and affect approximately one-quarter of adult cancer patients. Historically, intracranial metastatic disease conferred an exceptionally poor prognosis with median survival of approximately 1 month without treatment. Whole brain radiation therapy (WBRT) and aggressive supportive care extended median survival to 3–6 months ( 1 – 3 ). Significantly more prolonged survival is now possible in select subgroups of brain metastases patients ( 4 ). With prolonged survival, toxicities assume greater significance. Unfortunately, the majority of patients with brain metastases experience deterioration in neurocognitive function (NCF) after WBRT, raising concerns about toxicity ( 5 ). Several mechanisms have been identified by which irradiation may damage the brain and adversely impact NCF including vascular damage ( 6 – 7 ), glutamate dependent NMDA-mediated excitotoxicity ( 8 ), decreased neurogenesis ( 9 – 11 ), neuroinflammation and microglial activation ( 9 , 11 – 14 ), oxidative stress ( 15 ), and altered neuronal morphology and function ( 16 – 19 ). The vascular hypothesis of radiation injury proposes that brain irradiation leads to accelerated atherosclerosis and mineralizing microangiopathy, which in turn results in increased extracellular glutamate and subsequent N -methyl-d-aspartate (NMDA) receptor mediated excitotoxicity. Memantine, FDA approved for the treatment of Alzheimer’s and vascular dementia, is a noncompetitive antagonist of NMDA receptors that preferentially acts in the excitotoxic state by binding to NMDA receptors and preventing glutamate from binding thereby inhibiting the prolonged influx of Ca 2+ ions and neuronal excitotoxicity ( 20 – 23 ). NRG Oncology’s RTOG 0614 examined the efficacy of memantine to prevent radiation induced NCF decline in adult patients with brain metastases treated with 37.5 Gy of WBRT (+/− memantine) and demonstrated that patients treated with WBRT+memantine experienced prolongation of onset to NCF failure compared to patients treated with WBRT+placebo while achieving similar progression free survival and overall survival, and without increased toxicity ( 24 ). While many patients experience treatment related NCF decline after brain irradiation, the rate and magnitude of such decline is not uniform across individuals. This has led to interest in identifying risk and/or protective factors that may serve to further guide therapeutic decision making. Variations in germline genetic polymorphisms have been associated with NCF outcomes in numerous neurologic diseases as well as CNS and non-CNS cancer ( 25 ). One of the more commonly studied genes associated with NCF is the apolipoprotein E (APOE) gene, which is located on chromosome 19. APOE is involved in the uptake, transport and distribution of lipid, is expressed at high levels in the brain, is believed to play an important role in neuronal repair, and comes in three alleles (e2, e3, e4) ( 26 – 27 ). NCF dysfunction in APOE e4 carriers may arise through a variety of pathways including increased cerebral amyloid and tau burden, reduced cholinergic function, and dysregulated neuronal repair and glucose and phospholipid metabolism ( 28 ). APOE e4 carriers demonstrate more rapid age-related memory decline ( 29 ) and the e4 allele of the APOE gene is a well-established risk factor for sporadic Alzheimer’s disease (AD)( 30 – 31 ), with heterozygote APOE e4 carriers having a 2- to 4-fold increased risk and homozygote APOE e4 carriers having a 8- to 12-fold increased risk for AD ( 32 ). APOE e4 carriers show greater adverse NCF outcomes in other neurologic conditions such as traumatic brain injury ( 33 ) and stroke ( 34 ). In the setting of cancer and cancer therapy, APOE e4 carriers with breast cancer have also been found to exhibit greater NCF decline after chemotherapy compared to APOE e4 non-carriers ( 35 – 36 ). In a heterogeneous cross-sectional sample of brain tumor survivors evaluated retrospectively after treatment, APOE e4 carriers exhibited lower performance on tests of memory and executive function compared to APOE e4 carriers ( 37 ). In contrast to APOE e4, the e2 allele is believed to confer possible neuroprotection while the e3 allele is relatively more neutral in terms of risk of cognitive decline particularly in the most common neurodegenerative disease in which APOE has been studied, Alzheimer’s disease( 38 ). To determine if APOE e4 genotype is associated with baseline NCF or radiation-induced NCF decline in patients with brain metastases treated with WBRT, we examined the relationship between APOE carrier status and longitudinal changes in NCF in patients enrolled on NRG/RTOG 0614. MATERIALS AND METHODS This study was approved by the National Cancer Institute. The institutional review boards of the participating institutions approved the study protocol, and all patients provided written informed consent. All participants in this study were enrolled on NRG/RTOG 0614. Adult patients with a pathologically proven diagnosis of solid malignancy within 5 years of registration and with brain metastases visible on contrast-enhanced MRI (or a contrast-enhanced CT for patients unable to have an MRI) were eligible for NRG/RTOG 0614. Eligibility criteria included a Karnofsky performance status of ≥70, stable systemic disease in the 3 months prior to study entry, serum creatinine ≤3 mg/dL, creatinine clearance ≥30 mL/min, total bilirubin ≤2.5 mg/dL, blood urea nitrogen (BUN) <20 mg/dL, Mini Mental State Exam score >18, negative serum pregnancy test, no memantine allergy, no current alcohol or drug abuse, no chronic use of benzodiazepines, and no severe active comorbidity. Patients could have received prior therapy for brain metastasis, including radiosurgery and resection (but no prior cranial radiotherapy). Patients receiving systemic therapy were eligible if such therapy was given14 days prior to study entry, and they could not receive chemotherapy for at least 14 days after completing radiotherapy. NRG/RTOG 0614 stratified patients according to RPA class (class I vs II) and prior surgical therapy (none vs radiosurgery or resection within 8 weeks of randomization) and randomized patients to 37.5 Gy of WBRT (15 fractions of 2.5 Gy) with memantine or placebo. Patients were allowed to have received up to 3 WBRT treatments before starting study therapy (i.e. memantine or placebo) as long as WBRT parameters met protocol requirements. Patients completed NCF testing utilizing the Clinical Trial Battery [CTB] (Hopkins Verbal Learning Test-Revised [HVLT-R] ( 39 ), Trail Making Test [TMT] ( 40 ), Controlled Oral Word Association [COWA] ( 41 )) at baseline, 8, 16, 24, and 52 weeks. All NCF test administrators were trained and certified by the study Neurocognitive Chair (JSW). Raw NCF test scores were converted to standardized z-scores based on published normative data for each test ( 39 – 41 ). The CTB Composite (CTB COMP) score was calculated by averaging across all standardized z scores for the HVLT-R, TMT, and COWA tests combined. Patients that consented to an optional blood draw were included in this analysis. APOE alleles were evaluated by real-time PCR based SNP analysis using TaqMan genotyping adapted from Calero et al ( 42 ). Genomic DNA was extracted from 500ml of buffy coat by using the Master Pure Complete DNA and RNA Purification kit (EPICENTER) according to the manufacturer’s instructions. DNA was diluted with nuclease free water to 15 ng/μL for APOE genotyping analysis. TaqMan assay for APOE genotyping for the single nucleotide polymorphisms at rs429358 (112c/t) GCTGGGCGCGGACATGGAGGACGTG[C/T]GCGGCCGCCTGGTGCAGTACCGCGG and rs7412 (158c/t) CCGCGATGCCGATGACCTGCAGAAG[C/T]GCCTGGCAGTGTACCAGGCCGGGGC were used. The TaqMan technique combines DNA amplification and genotype detection in a single assay. The method involves two types of oligonucleotide probes, one for each allele, which are designed to hybridize to a region of the single-stranded DNA templates that contains the allele-specific nucleotide. Fluorogenic reporter dyes, 6-carboxyfluorescein (FAM) and VIC) are attached to the 5’ ends of the probes for allele 1 and allele 2, respectively. (Applied Biosystems). The reactions were carried out on a 96-well microtiter plates and the om a volume of 22 μl, containing 11 μl of 2X TaqMan Universal PCR Master Mix (Applied Biosystems), 100 nM each of the FAM-labeled and VIC-labeled probe, and 2 μl of DNA (15 ng). Reactions were conducted with 40 cycles of denaturation at 95 °C for 15 seconds and primer hybridization and extension at 60 °C for 1 minute. Automated genotype calling was carried out on the ViiA7 Sequence Detection System equipped with the allelic discrimination analysis module (software version 1.2.1; Applied Biosystems). Descriptive statistics were run to examine sociodemographic and clinical characteristics. Chi-square test, or Fisher’s exact test if cell counts are small, for categorical variables or the t-test for continuous variables were employed to compare pretreatment characteristics between patients with and without APOE e4 data. Baseline NCF (HVLT-R Total Recall [TR], Delayed Recall [DR], and Delayed Recognition [RECOG], TMT Part A [TMT-A] and B [TMT-B], COWA, and the CTB COMP) was compared between carriers and non-carriers of an APOE e4 allele using the t-test, or Wilcoxon test if not normally distributed. NCF test results were evaluated over time with a repeated measures mixed effects model with random intercept using maximum likelihood estimation, which is valid under the data missing at random missing data mechanism, adjusted for baseline NCF test score, treatment arm, APOE carrier status, an interaction between time and APOE carrier status to determine the effect of APOE carrier status on the repeated NCF test across time, and variables found to significantly differ by APOE status. Quadratic and cubic time effects were considered to improve the fit of the models based on their significance at a 0.10 level. Multiple imputation was performed on all alive patients missing NCF tests using the Markov chain Monte Carlo method and 10 imputations. The mixed effects models were run using the imputed data under the data missing not at random mechanism. Time to NCF failure, defined as the first failure on any NCF test using each test’s Reliable Change Index (RCI) criterion ( 43 ), was assessed using cumulative incidence treating death as a competing risk. Patients that were APOE e4 carriers vs. APOE e4 non-carriers were tested using Gray’s test. Hazard ratios (HRs) and corresponding 95% confidence intervals (CIs) were calculated using cause-specific Cox models for time to NCF failure. Adjusted models incorporated treatment arm and a possible treatment by APOE carrier status interaction. RESULTS APOE genotype was available for 224 (44%) of the 508 subjects ( Figure 1 ). There were no significant differences between patients with and those without available biospecimens in any pretreatment sociodemographic or clinical characteristic ( supplemental Table 1 ). Of patients with specimens available, 29.5% (n=66) were APOE e4 carriers (e4/e4 n=5, e3/e4 n= 53, e2/e4 n=8). APOE e4 carriers were distributed evenly among the treatment arms (35 patients in the memantine arm and 31 in the placebo arm, p=0.24). The distribution of APOE e4 non-carriers were: 56.3% (n=126) e3/e3, 11.2% (n=25) e2/e3, and 3.1% (n=7) e2/e2. APOE e4 carriers, when compared to APOE e4 non-carriers, were less often white (82% vs 89%, respectively, p=0.0077) and had started WBRT at the time of study entry more frequently (39% vs 21%, respectively, p=0.0041) ( Table 1 ). Patients in both groups were predominantly white, had a lung cancer primary, and did not differ on any NCF test at baseline with test performance generally in the mild to moderately impaired range compared to normative comparison groups. Figure 1. Open in a new tab CONSORT diagram Table 1. Pretreatment Patient Sociodemographic and Clinical Characteristics APOE e4 APOE e4 non-carrier (n=160) carrier (n=67) † p-value Age (years) Median 59 60 0.66 § Min - Max 29 – 85 33 – 84 Q1 - Q3 51 – 65 52 – 66 Gender Male 72 (45.0%) 32 (47.8%) 0.70 Female 88 (55.0%) 35 (52.2%) Race White vs. Other 0.04 American Indian/Alaska Native 0 (0.0%) 1 (1.5%) Asian 3 (1.9%) 3 (4.5%) Black or African American 12 (7.5%) 9 (13.4%) White 142 (88.8%) 54 (80.6%) Unknown or not reported 3 (1.9%) 0 (0.0%) Education G0–11/HS/GED 99 (61.9%) 44 (65.7%) 0.42 Some College/Technical School 33 (20.6%) 9 (13.4%) Bachelor’s Degree 28 (17.5%) 14 (20.9%) Ethnicity Not Hispanic vs. Other 0.21 Hispanic or Latino 4 (2.5%) 4 (6.0%) Not Hispanic or Latino 151 (94.4%) 63 (94.0%) Unknown (Individuals not reporting ethnicity) 5 (3.1%) 0 (0.0%) Neurologic Function Status No symptoms vs. at least some 0.77 No symptoms, fully active 63 (39.4%) 25 (37.3%) Minor symptoms 71 (44.4%) 30 (44.8%) Moderate symptoms, fully active 15 (