Delayed Recognition, D) TMTB, E) CTB COMP. HVLT-R=Hopkins Verbal Learning Test – Revised; TMTB=Trail Making Test Part B; CTB COMP=Clinical Trial Battery Composite. APOE genotype was not associated with acceleration of time to NCF failure (HR=0.87, 95% CI: 0.61–1.24, p=0.45; Table 3 ). The results were similar for the adjusted analysis (HR=0.91, 95% CI: 0.63–1.30, p=0.59), which also showed treatment with memantine trended to delay time to NCF failure in this subset of patients with available genetic data (HR=0.74, 95% CI: 0.53–1.03, p=0.077; Table 4 ) and prior WBRT was not associated with time to NCF failure (HR=0.74, 95% CI: 0.51–1.09, p=0.13; Table 4 ). Table 3. Univariate Cause-Specific Cox Model for Time to NCF Failure Variables Hazard Ratio 95%CI P-value APOE status (APOE e4 non- carrier vs. APOE e4 carrier [RL]) 0.878 (0.62 – 1.25) 0.47 Patients Lost to Cognitive Failure /Total 143/224 Patients Lost to Death 64 Open in a new tab RL=Reference level; WBRT=whole-brain radiotherapy. Table 4. Multivariable Cause-Specific Cox Model for Time to NCF Failure Variables Hazard Ratio 95%CI P-value Treatment Arm (Memantine vs. Placebo [RL]) 0.74 (0.53 – 1.03) 0.077 APOE status (APOE e4 non- carrier vs. APOE e4 carrier [RL]) 0.91 (0.63 – 1.30) 0.59 Prior WBRT (No vs. Yes [RL]) 0.74 (0.51 – 1.09) 0.13 Patients Lost to Cognitive Failure/Total 143/224 Patients Lost to Death 64 Open in a new tab Interaction between treatment arm and APOE status was considered but removed from the model as it was not significant. RL=Reference level; WBRT=whole-brain radiotherapy. DISCUSSION Patients that receive brain irradiation are at increased risk for NCF decline secondary to treatment. NCF decline after WBRT is a frequent occurrence for many patients. NRG/RTOG 0614 demonstrated that the addition of memantine to WBRT improved NCF outcomes compared to placebo. However, some patients still exhibit NCF decline and no clear risk factors have been identified to help predict which patients are at greatest risk. To our knowledge, NRG/RTOG 0614 is the first prospective, longitudinal, randomized controlled trial that collected patient blood for genotyping to evaluate APOE carrier status as a risk factor for differential NCF toxicity after WBRT. Patients carrying the APOE e4 allele compared to APOE e4 non-carriers did not exhibit any differences in NCF at baseline prior to WBRT. While there was slight imbalance between the groups with more APOE e4 carriers completing baseline testing after having started WBRT APOE e4 carriers exhibited greater NCF decline after WBRT (+/− memantine) in learning and memory, executive function, and the CTB composite score, and these results did not change with adjustment for prior WBRT treatment. These NCF domains parallel those reported to decline with age in ―healthy‖ carriers of the APOE e4 allele ( 29 ). Notably, APOE e4 carriers treated with WBRT declined across all aspects of episodic learning and memory processes including encoding, consolidation, and retrieval processes. These marked effects across all episodic learning and memory processes are consistent with reports of accelerated hippocampal atrophy ( 44 ) and the clear increased risk for memory disorders such as Alzheimer’s disease ( 32 ) in APOE e4 carriers. Moreover, ―healthy‖ older APOE e4 carriers have been reported to show regional brain abnormalities thought to represent accelerated neural aging that underlie deficits in learning and memory processes including reduced hippocampal volume ( 29 , 45 ) and breakdown of typical associations between mesial temporal lobe functional activation, structural characteristics and NCF ( 46 ). Similarly, APOE e4 carriers have been found to exhibit structural and functional brain network abnormalities that correlate with episodic learning and memory function specifically, and also more broadly across multiple NCF domains ( 47 – 48 ). In the future, brain metastasis studies that examine both genetic variation and imaging features may be able to further refine the prediction of risk for NCF decline after radiation and increase our understanding of the brain regions and networks susceptible to radiation damage. Such information may help guide future approaches to risk mitigation including patient selection, risk-adjusted therapy, and radiation treatment contouring to avoid brain regions that are sensitive to radiation toxicity and critical for NCF. As in Alzheimer’s disease ( 32 ), treatment-related NCF decline in patients with brain tumor has been associated with many genes indicating evidence that this is also a polygenic disorder ( 49 – 50 ). As such, broader genotyping studies will be helpful in the future and will need to consider risk of Type-1 error that accompanies analyses of multiple risk/protective factors. Kuo et al. ( 55 ) recently reported that APOE e4 carriers exhibited greater decline in memory function after hippocampal avoidant WBRT. Whereas patients that were APOE e2 carriers exhibited the greatest preservation of cognitive function after WBRT. In contrast to the findings of the current study and those of Kuo et al., a secondary analysis of NCCTG N107C/CEC.3 reported by Huntoon et al. ( 51 ), failed to identify associations between APOE e4 carrier status and cognitive decline at 3 months after treatment for brain metastases. There are a number of differences between Huntoon et al. and the current study that may have contributed to differences in associations between APOE e4 and cognitive outcomes reported by the two studies: ( 1 ) Huntoon et al evaluated patients treated with WBRT (n=82) or SRS (n=93) while the current study reported on a much larger sample of patients uniformly treated with WBRT (n=227), moreover, a greater adverse impact on cognition is expected in the WBRT treated population affording greater power to see gene*outcome effects; ( 2 ) only 43 patients (28 in the WBRT arm) were carries of the APOE e4 allele in the Huntoon et al study, whereas the current study had 67 patients treated with WBRT that were APOE e4 allele carriers, again likely resulting in increased power to identify associations with cognitive outcomes; ( 3 ) Huntoon et al examined the association between APOE carrier status and cognitive decline at a single 3 month timepoint based on a dichotomous criterion for cognitive decline that included 1 SD decline on any test, death or missing data, whereas we examined the association between APOE carrier status and longitudinal change in cognitive function over time measured as a continuous variable, which again provides greater statistical power to detect associations and does not confound declaration of cognitive change with death or missing data; and ( 4 ) it is likely that the brain metastasis populations enrolled on the two studies differed as NCCTG N107C/CEC.3 required patients to be eligible for postoperative SRS whereas this was not the case for RTOG 0614. Treatment with memantine was found to delay time to NCF failure with no statistically significant differences between patients with and without an APOE e4 allele. APOE e4 carrier status appears to be a risk factor for NCF decline in patients with brain metastases treated with WBRT regardless of treatment with memantine. The lack of an interaction between APOE carrier status and the memantine arm brings up the interesting possibility that the mechanisms by which APOE e4 carrier status increases risk for NCF decline may be independent of NMDA mediated excitotoxicity. APOE e4 carriers compared to APOE e4 non-carriers show greater amyloid beta and tau burden in middle age and greater accumulation rates over time in medial temporal regions, which have been associated with increased NCF dysfunction in aging and MCI/AD ( 52 ). It is unclear if brain irradiation potentiates amyloid beta or tau burden. Clarifying this would shed light on other potential therapeutic avenues such as those being investigated for neurodegenerative disease including drugs targeting amyloid beta-protein and tau-protein. This study is not without limitations including the fact that as an optional procedure not all patients participated in the blood draw for genetic analyses. However, there was no identifiable imbalance in demographic or clinical factors suggesting these findings are likely generalizable to the larger study population. The sample size of APOE e4 carriers limits power especially for APOE e4 homozygotes and for categorical outcome analyses such as time to NCF failure, yet it is currently the largest sample size to evaluate this association in this patient population. Relatedly, missing data over time creates challenges for analyses and generalizability of findings. Analyses were conducted using multiple imputation as a sensitivity analysis to check the robustness of the findings from the complete case analysis. While these suggested no effect of APOE carrier status it is likely that missing NCF data over time is due to a missing not at random process (e.g., increased symptomatology) that may introduce bias and contribute to misleading results ( 53 ). Given that both “complete” case analysis in the context of missing data and multiple imputation can result in biased findings, and these two analyses did not coalesce into a common finding in this study, further investigation of these associations are warranted. Given the importance of understanding NCF outcomes in brain metastases populations and genetic risk/protective factors that may help guide and personalize therapeutic decision making, routine inclusion of NCF outcomes with optimized data collection efforts and informative coding of reasons for missing data, and blood draws for genetic analyses are encouraged in future trials. Consistent with the typical race distribution seen in participants in cooperative group trials, the patient population was predominantly Caucasian. Efforts to increase the diversity of cancer patients that participate in cooperative group clinical trials has been previously highlighted ( 54 ). Determining if the relationship between APOE carrier status and NCF decline after WBRT is the same for non-Caucasians requires further investigation. More recently WBRT is increasingly delivered with avoidance of the hippocampal region. Studies such as NRG CC001 collected plasma and serum in addition to cognitive outcomes affording the opportunity to evaluate the influence of APOE carrier status on cognitive function after this therapeutic approach and validate the findings of Kuo et al.. NRG/RTOG 0614 represents the first large study that prospectively and longitudinally objectively measured NCF and identified a genetic risk factor, APOE e4 carrier status, associated with worse NCF outcomes in a patient population uniformly treated with WBRT (+/− memantine) for brain metastases. This represents the most robust evidence that APOE e4 carrier status may be a risk factor for adverse NCF outcomes after WBRT, although validation in future studies is needed. Furthermore, this is proof of principle that studies integrating novel treatment strategies, state of the art NCF monitoring, and advanced biomarker endpoints, even in a population with high morbidity and mortality, can and should occur within the cooperative group setting. Such comprehensive studies could help guide investigations and discussions about risk adjusted treatment, clinical trial selection/enrichment, and the development of biologically informed adjunctive therapeutics. Supplementary Material 1 NIHMS1980152-supplement-1.docx (87.6KB, docx) Funding Source: This project was supported by grants U10CA180868 (NRG Oncology Operations), U10CA180822 (NRG Oncology SDMC), UG1CA189867 (NCORP) from the National Cancer Institute (NCI), Brains Together for a Cure, and Allergan. Footnotes Conflict of Interest Statement: Drs. Brown, Bruner, Cerhan, Chen, Curtis, Deshmukh, Elsayed, Grosshans, Laack, Laba, Pugh report they have no conflicts if interest. Dr. Khuntia reports employmen