INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(269,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10238259','ASTRO''s Advances in Radiation Oncology''s Top 20 Downloads for 2022.','Miller RC, Tsai CJ.','2023','astro reirradiation hypofractionation hypofractionation reirradiation astro reirradiation fattahi rasmusson pt2n0r0 abscopal ultrahypofractionated reirradiation rulach bedi duriseti enzalutamide leuprolide nonmetastatic shee influencers thrower quad nonosseous kil pearlman fappiano fattahi reirradiation eirradiation reirradiation lthough irradiating allowances programmable neurostimulators shunts recorders citated citescore astro zaorsky braunstein krengli hassanzadeh citescore astro astro astro astro varian nanobiotix fattahi reirradiation fmr bernabe gelblum rasmusson kjellen gunnlaugsson barsky pt2n0r0','Our top downloads for the ASTRO''s Advances in Radiation Oncology in 2022 were focused on a variety of topics, reflecting shifting interests in our profession with reirradiation, hypofractionation, and symptom management to improve both the short-term and long-term quality of life of our patients. Table 1 contains a list and links to our 20 most downloaded articles in 2022. Approximately half of our top 20 downloads relate to hypofractionation and reirradiation/symptom management. Although we continue to publish a handful of COVID-19-related articles, this topic no longer has the clinical urgency that was seen in 2020 and 2021. Table 1. Top 20 downloads from ASTRO''s Advances in Radiation Oncology during 2022 DOI address Article title First author https://doi.org/10.1016/j.adro.2020.100640 Reirradiation for Locoregional Recurrent Breast Cancer Fattahi, S. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.adro.2022.100912 Interventions for Radiation-Induced Fibrosis in Patients With Breast Cancer: Systematic Review and Meta-analyses Nogueira, R. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.adro.2021.100732 A Review and Analysis of Managing Commonly Seen Implanted Devices for Patients Undergoing Radiation Therapy Chan, M. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.adro.2020.09.011 Long-Term Risk of Hip Complications After Radiation Therapy for Prostate Cancer: A Dose-Response Study Rasmusson, E. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.adro.2018.09.006 The Perineural Invasion Paradox: Is Perineural Invasion an Independent Prognostic Indicator of Biochemical Recurrence Risk in Patients With pT2N0R0 Prostate Cancer? A Multi-Institutional Study Kraus, R. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.adro.2022.100909 A Patient-Level Data Meta-analysis of the Abscopal Effect Hatten, S. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.adro.2021.100877 Adoption of Ultrahypofractionated Radiation Therapy in Patients With Breast Cancer Corrigan, K. J. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.adro.2021.100653 An International Expert Survey on the Indications and Practice of Radical Thoracic Reirradiation for Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer Rulach, R. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.adro.2021.100850 Is 5 the New 25? Long-Term Oncologic Outcomes From a Phase II, Prospective, 5-Fraction Preoperative Radiation Therapy Trial in Patients With Localized Soft Tissue Sarcoma Bedi, M. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.adro.2020.100639 Spatially Fractionated Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy (Lattice) for Large Tumors Duriseti, S. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.adro.2022.100941 Addition of Enzalutamide to Leuprolide and Definitive Radiation Therapy Is Tolerable and Effective in High-Risk Localized or Regional Nonmetastatic Prostate Cancer: Results From a Phase 2 Trial Shee, K. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.adro.2022.100919 Characterizing Twitter Influencers in Radiation Oncology Valle, L. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.adro.2021.100708 The Effect of Slice Thickness on Contours of Brain Metastases for Stereotactic Radiosurgery Thrower, S. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.adro.2020.09.016 Evaluation of Mobile Health Applications to Track Patient-Reported Outcomes for Oncology Patients: A Systematic Review Lu, D. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.adro.2021.100871 Rapid and Durable Symptom Palliation With Quad Shot Radiation Therapy to Nonosseous Metastatic/Recurrent Cancer in Elderly or Frail Patients in a Rural Community Clinic Kil, W. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.adro.2021.100878 Long-Term Outcomes for Patients With Atypical or Malignant Meningiomas Treated With or Without Radiation Therapy: A 25-Year Retrospective Analysis of a Single-Institution Experience Kent, C. W. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.adro.2022.100906 Clinical Outcomes of Dose-Escalated Hypofractionated External Beam Radiation Therapy (5 Gy × 5 Fractions) for Spine Metastasis Shin, J. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.adro.2021.100723 Adverse Effects of Total Body Irradiation: A Two-Decade, Single Institution Analysis Pearlman, R. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.adro.2021.100713 Hydrogel Spacer Rectal Wall Infiltration Associated With Severe Rectal Injury and Related Complications After Dose Intensified Prostate Cancer Stereotactic Ablative Radiation Therapy McLaughlin, M. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.adro.2022.100913 Hypofractionated Radiation Therapy for Unresectable or Metastatic Sarcoma Lesions Boyce-Fappiano, D. Open in a new tab Our most downloaded article, by Fattahi et al, continues to be the Mayo Clinic study on breast reirradiation. This article reported outcomes that suggest that “[r]eirradiation for locoregional recurrent breast cancer is feasible with acceptable rates of toxicity. Disease control and survival are promising among curative intent reirradiation patients without gross disease.” 1 Our second most downloaded article, from Chan et al, also focused on breast cancer and was a systematic review and meta-analysis of the literature on radiation-induced fibrosis after breast radiation. The authors suggested that “[a]lthough isolated studies show significant results favorable to the experimental groups, caution should be exercised in these findings because of the small number, small sample size, and high risk of bias presented by some of the included studies, which makes the recommendation for clinical practice still weak.” 2 Coming in at number three was a review of issues associated with irradiating patients with implantable medical devices, an area where allowances must be made for a wide variety of devices that now include not only cardiac pacemakers but also “implanted cardiac defibrillators, programmable hepatic pumps, pain pumps, neurostimulators, cerebral shunts, and loop recorders.” The article includes a sample workflow incorporating each of these devices, with risk categories based on AAPM TG-203. 3 Our fourth and fifth most downloaded articles are related to prostate cancer, the fourth focusing on the long-term risk of hip complications 4 and the fifth on the prognostic value of perineural invasion in patients with pT 2 N 0 R 0 prostate cancer. 5 Our journal continues to grow in readership and citations. Table 2 contains our top 5 most citated articles in 2022. We anticipate receiving an Impact Factor later this year. Currently, our CiteScore, an alternative measure of impact using 4 years of data rather than data from the previous 2 years that Impact Factor measures, is 4.3 ( Fig. 1 ). With a total 6266 citations of our journal articles published between 2016 and 2022, Advances is the global leader among open-access radiation journals for number of citations ( Fig. 2 ). As an open-access journal, Advances is always freely available to any health care professional or researcher in the world. It is also freely available to our patients, their families, and patient advocacy groups around the world ( Fig. 3 ). Table 2. Top cited articles in ASTRO''s Advances in Radiation Oncology in the past 3 years DOI address Article title First author Cited https://doi.org/10.1016/j.adro.2020.03.010 Prostate Cancer Radiation Therapy Recommendations in Response to COVID-19 Zaorsky, N. G. 129 https://doi.org/10.1016/j.adro.2020.03.013 Breast Radiation Therapy Under COVID-19 Pandemic Resource Constraints—Approaches to Defer or Shorten Treatment From a Comprehensive Cancer Center in the United States Braunstein, L. Z. 77 https://doi.org/10.1016/j.adro.2020.03.003 Running a Radiation Oncology Department at the Time of Coronavirus: An Italian Experience Krengli, M. 55 https://doi.org/10.1016/j.adro.2020.06.010 Ablative Five-Fraction Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy for Inoperable Pancreatic Cancer Using Online MR-Guided Adaptation Hassanzadeh, C. 49 https://doi.org/10.1016/j.adro.2020.03.006 The Impact of COVID-19 on Radiation Oncology Clinics and Patients With Cancer in the United States Rivera, A. 48 Open in a new tab Figure 1. Open in a new tab CiteScore ranking for ASTRO''s Advances in Radiation Oncology from 2017 to 2021. Figure 2. Open in a new tab Growth in citations per year for ASTRO''s Advances in Radiation Oncology from 2015 to 2022. Figure 3. Open in a new tab Global distribution of ASTRO''s Advances in Radiation Oncology during 2022. Footnotes Sources of support: This work had no specific funding. Disclosures: Drs Miller and Tsai report honoraria from ASTRO. Dr Tsai reports consulting fees from Varian Medical Inc and Nanobiotix. No other disclosures were reported. References 1. Fattahi S, Ahmed SK, Park SS, et al. Reirradiation for locoregional recurrent breast cancer. Adv Radiat Oncol. 2020;6 doi: 10.1016/j.adro.2020.100640. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Nogueira RMP, Vital FMR, Bernabé DG, Carvalho MB. Interventions for radiation-induced fibrosis in patients with breast cancer: Systematic review and meta-analyses. Adv Radiat Oncol. 2022;7 doi: 10.1016/j.adro.2022.100912. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Chan MF, Young C, Gelblum D, et al. A review and analysis of managing commonly seen implanted devices for patients undergoing radiation therapy. Adv Radiat Oncol. 2021;6 doi: 10.1016/j.adro.2021.100732. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Rasmusson E, Nilsson P, Kjellén E, Gunnlaugsson A. Long-term risk of hip complications after radiation therapy for prostate cancer: A dose-response study. Adv Radiat Oncol. 2020;6 doi: 10.1016/j.adro.2020.09.011. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Kraus RD, Barsky A, Ji L, et al. The perineural invasion paradox: Is perineural invasion an independent prognostic indicator of biochemical recurrence risk in patients with pT2N0R0 prostate cancer? A multi-institutional study. Adv Radiat Oncol. 2018;4:96–102. doi: 10.1016/j.adro.2018.09.006. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',23,'2025-10-03 13:26:33','2025-10-03 13:26:33');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(270,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11792186','Benchmarking LLM chatbots'' oncological knowledge with the Turkish Society of Medical Oncology''s annual board examination questions.','Erdat EC, Kavak EE.','2025','underexplored sonnet anthropic sonnet sonnet sonnet anthropic tıbbi onkoloji dernegi typos punctuation grammatical chats sonnet anthropic 70b raspberry raspberry chats sonnet lllama lllama hsd sonnet sonnet respectable sonnet lllama sonnet sonnet sonnet sonnet nuri karadurmus unconditional conceptalizuation heacock hentel reig edged swords okuhara shirabe nishiie kiuchi e60807 dobbs hutchings e179 meropol schrag mulvey langdon ubel schnipper krittanawong rosenson aydar baber whw ehz056 esteva kuprel novoa swetter blau thrun nature21056 erdat yalciner urun fon safranek socrates chartash e45312 kasapovic kaczmarczyk e46482 lahat zoabi shneor patt shani arow klang e54571 zegarra nana cordova arteaga cisneros chalco xcb cjg godoy kltp arratia cohaila jeehp lonergan dhas btz682 cheatham medenilla sillos elepano madriaga aggabao candido maningo e0000198 pdig s12032','Abstract Background Large language models (LLMs) have shown promise in various medical applications, including clinical decision-making and education. In oncology, the increasing complexity of patient care and the vast volume of medical literature require efficient tools to assist practitioners. However, the use of LLMs in oncology education and knowledge assessment remains underexplored. This study aims to evaluate and compare the oncological knowledge of four LLMs using standardized board examination questions. Methods We assessed the performance of four LLMs—Claude 3.5 Sonnet (Anthropic), ChatGPT 4o (OpenAI), Llama-3 (Meta), and Gemini 1.5 (Google)—using the Turkish Society of Medical Oncology’s annual board examination questions from 2016 to 2024. A total of 790 valid multiple-choice questions covering various oncology topics were included. Each model was tested on its ability to answer these questions in Turkish. Performance was analyzed based on the number of correct answers, with statistical comparisons made using chi-square tests and one-way ANOVA. Results Claude 3.5 Sonnet outperformed the other models, passing all eight exams with an average score of 77.6%. ChatGPT 4o passed seven out of eight exams, with an average score of 67.8%. Llama-3 and Gemini 1.5 showed lower performance, passing four and three exams respectively, with average scores below 50%. Significant differences were observed among the models’ performances (F = 17.39, p < 0.001). Claude 3.5 and ChatGPT 4.0 demonstrated higher accuracy across most oncology topics. A decline in performance in recent years, particularly in the 2024 exam, suggests limitations due to outdated training data. Conclusions Significant differences in oncological knowledge were observed among the four LLMs, with Claude 3.5 Sonnet and ChatGPT 4o demonstrating superior performance. These findings suggest that advanced LLMs have the potential to serve as valuable tools in oncology education and decision support. However, regular updates and enhancements are necessary to maintain their relevance and accuracy, especially to incorporate the latest medical advancements. Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1186/s12885-025-13596-0. Keywords: Artificial intelligence, Large language models, Oncology, Clinical decision support systems, Medical education, Board examinations Background The rapid advancement of artificial intelligence (AI) and natural language processing has led to the development of large language models (LLMs) capable of understanding and generating human-like text with high intellectual ability. Models such as OpenAI’s GPT-3 and GPT-4 have demonstrated remarkable proficiency in various applications, including language translation, content creation, and even passing standardized exams [ 1 ]. In the medical field, LLMs have shown potential in assisting with clinical decision-making, patient education, and streamlining administrative tasks [ 2 , 3 ]. In oncology, the volume of medical literature and the complexity of patient care are ever-increasing. Oncologists must stay updated with the latest research findings, clinical guidelines, and treatment modalities to provide optimal patient care [ 4 ]. The integration of AI in oncology has been explored for tasks such as image interpretation, genomics, treatment planning and predicting treatment outcomes [ 5 , 6 , 7 ]. However, applying LLMs comprehensively in oncology education and knowledge assessment remains relatively unexplored. Previous studies have evaluated the general medical knowledge of AI models. For instance, GPT-3 was tested on the United States Medical Licensing Examination (USMLE) questions and showed promising results, and more recently, GPT-4 demonstrated even higher accuracy on various medical exam questions, indicating rapid progress in the capabilities of LLMs [ 2 , 8 ]. Despite these advancements, few studies have specifically assessed the proficiency of LLMs in oncology. The Turkish Society of Medical Oncology’s annual board examinations provide a comprehensive assessment of oncological knowledge across multiple subspecialties. These exams cover topics ranging from basic oncology principles to specific tumor types and palliative care. Utilizing these examinations as an evaluation tool offers an objective measure of the LLMs’ capabilities in a specialized medical field. This study aims to evaluate and compare the oncological knowledge of four LLMs—Claude 3.5 Sonnet (Anthropic), ChatGPT 4o (OpenAI), Llama-3 (Meta), and Gemini 1.5 (Google)—using the Turkish Society of Medical Oncology’s board examination questions from 2016 to 2024. By analyzing their performance across different oncology topics and over several years, we aim to provide insights into the current state of AI in oncology and identify areas for improvement. The findings could have significant implications for the future integration of AI tools in oncology education and clinical practice. Materials and methods Questions and data inputs After receiving approval from the Turkish Society of Medical Oncology (Türk Tıbbi Onkoloji Derneği), we gathered eight consecutive annual examination questions between 2016 and 2024 from the official website of the society. Each annual exam consisted of 100 multiple-choice questions covering different oncological topics, each with a single correct answer. Due to the COVID-19 pandemic, the board examination for 2020 was not conducted; therefore, eight annual board examinations totaling 800 questions were included in the study. As all questions were text-based, none were excluded due to figures or tables; only questions that were canceled due to no valid answers were excluded, resulting in 790 valid questions. We transferred each year’s questions to dedicated text files and double-checked to ensure they were in the correct order. Each question was categorized into main oncology topics, and the correct answers were entered into spreadsheets. Data preparation and encoding We edited each question to eliminate typos and unintentional punctuation, ensuring that all questions were in the same format and included the question stem and all available choices. The questions’ grammatical or structural errors were not corrected; all questions remained in Turkish. Before administering the questions, we confirmed in separate chats that the LLMs—Claude 3.5 Sonnet (Anthropic), ChatGPT 4o (OpenAI), Gemini 1.5 (Google), and Llama-3-70b (Meta)—could understand Turkish correctly. New, separate chat sessions were then initiated for each model. For Llama-3, sessions were opened using Raspberry Pi 5 Model B, 8 GB version (Raspberry Foundation, UK). All chats began with the prompt: “I will give you some multiple-choice questions with one correct answer; you will tell me your answers,” followed by each question asked separately. If any flaws in encoding were observed, new chat sessions were opened, and the prompt was given again. The LLMs’ answers and the correct answers were transferred to spreadsheets for analysis. Data analysis We categorized the questions by cancer topics and years for further analysis. A passing score of 50 correct answers was used to determine if a model passed the test. Analyses included each model’s performance in different topics and different years. Differences among the performances of the models were analyzed using the chi-square test, and their overall scores were compared with a one-way ANOVA. The analyses were conducted using GraphPad Prism 10 (GraphPad Software, Boston, Massachusetts, USA). Results We included eight board examinations between 2016 and 2024, consisting of 790 questions in the study. The mean count of valid questions for board examinations was 98.8. The majority of board examinations consisted of basic oncology, 118 questions were about basic oncology, 75 were about thoracic tumors, 74 were about non-colorectal and colorectal tumors each, 73 were about breast tumors, 64 were about genitourinary tumors, 57 were about gynecological tumors and palliative care each, 40 were about hematological tumors and bone tumors-soft tissue sarcomas each, 39 were about head and neck tumors, 33 about other topics, 24 were about central nervous system tumors, and 22 were about skin tumors. The board examinations’ structure and mean valid questions per exam were presented in Table 1 . Table 1. The structure of board examinations, topics and mean valid questions per exam Category Question Count Mean valid questions per exam Basic oncology, epidemiology and statistics 118 14.8 Thoracic tumors 75 9.4 Non-colorectal gastrointestinal tumors 74 9.3 Colorectal tumors 74 9.3 Breast tumors 73 9.1 Genitourinary tumors 64 8 Gynecological tumors 57 7.1 Palliative care and end of life 57 7.1 Hematological tumors 40 5 Bone tumors and soft tissue sarcomas 40 5 Head and neck tumors 39 4.9 Other topics 33 4.1 Central nervous system tumors 24 3 Skin tumors 22 2.8 Total 790 98.8 Open in a new tab Claude 3.5 Sonnet outperformed all the other models. The model’s scores ranged from 67 to 86, with an average score 77.6 (SD = 7.59), passing all the 8 exams (ANOVA test, F = 96.79, p < 0.001). The model’s performance was decreased in consecutive years yet still above the passing score. ChatGPT 4o achieved scores above the passing threshold then Llama-3 and Gemini 1.5. The model’s scores ranged from 44 to 83 points, with an average score of 67.8 (SD = 14.3). ChatGPT 4o passed 7 out of 8 exams, failing only in 2024 when it scored 44 points, which was below the required 50 points for passing. Lllama-3 demonstrated more variability in performance, with scores ranging from 24 to 55 points. The model passed 4 out of 8 exams, with scores above the passing threshold in four exams between 2016 and 2019. The mean score for Lllama-3 was 40.2 (SD = 13.78). The model failed to achieve the passing score in the last four consecutive years, indicating a declining trend in performance. Gemini 1.5 also exhibited a mix of results, with scores ranging between 34 and 59 points. This model passed 3 out of the 8 exams, with passing scores in 2016, 2017, and 2018. The mean score for Gemini 1.5 was 47.3 (SD = 9.09). Similar to Llama-3, Gemini 1.5 failed to reach the passing score in several years, particularly towards the end of the study period. A one-way analysis of variance (ANOVA) was conducted to determine if there were statistically significant differences in exam scores among the three LLMs and there was a significant difference in performance among four LLMs among years, (ANOVA test, F = 19.31, p < 0.001). Post-hoc comparisons using Tukey’s HSD test revealed that the mean scores of Claude 3.5 Sonnet were significantly higher than ChatGPT 4o, Llama-3 and Gemini 1.5. ChatGPT 4o were significantly higher than those of Llama-3 and Gemini 1.5. There was no significant difference between the scores of Llama-3 and Gemini 1.5. The chi-square test showed no significant differences between passed exams among different LLMs ( p = 0.378). The scores for each year and the pass/fail outcomes for each LLM are presented in Table 2 . Table 2. Model performance by exams, passing status and points received Year Exam result, points (passed/failed) Claude 3.5 ChatGPT 4o Llama-3 Gemini 1.5 2016 86 (Passed) 82 (Passed) 53 (Passed) 54 (Passed) 2017 86 (Passed) 83 (Passed) 53 (Passed) 59 (Passed) 2018 84 (Passed) 82 (Passed) 55 (Passed) 59 (Passed) 2019 72 (Passed) 72 (Passed) 51 (Passed) 44 (Failed) 2021 73 (Passed) 64 (Passed) 27 (Failed) 40 (Failed) 2022 73.7 (Passed) 56.8 (Passed) 29.5 (Failed) 44.2 (Failed) 2023 70.5 (Passed) 58.9 (Passed) 29.5 (Failed) 44.2 (Failed) 2024 67 (Passed) 44 (Failed) 24 (Failed) 34 (Failed) Mean (SD) 76.5 (7.59) 67.8 (14.3) 40.2 (13.78) 47.3 (9.09) Passed exams 8/8 7/8 4/8 3/8 Open in a new tab Claude 3.5 Sonnet consistently outperformed the other models across all question categories (ANOVA test, F = 124.5, p < 0.001). In the category of “Basic oncology, epidemiology, and statistics,” Claude 3.5 answered 106 out of 118 questions correctly, achieving the highest accuracy rate of 89.8%. This was significantly better than the other models. Claude 3.5 also demonstrated exceptional performance in categories such as “Bone tumors and soft tissue sarcomas” (77.5% accuracy), “Palliative care and end of life” (77.2% accuracy), and “Other topics” (78.8% accuracy). In some categories, like “Skin tumors” (81.8% accuracy) and “Head and neck tumors” (71.8% accuracy), Claude 3.5 showed substantial strengths. The model’s lowest accuracy was in the “Genitourinary tumors” category, where it still achieved a respectable 70.3% accuracy, outperforming the majority of the other models. ChatGPT 4o fell behind Claude 3.5 Sonnet but outperformed the Llama-3 and Gemini 1.5 models across all question categories. In the category of “Basic oncology, epidemiology, and statistics”, ChatGPT 4o answered 102 out of 118 questions correctly, achieving the highest accuracy rate of 86.4%. In contrast, Llama-3 and Gemini 1.5 had accuracy rates of 64.4% and 60.2%, respectively. ChatGPT 4o also demonstrated significant performance in categories such as “Bone tumors and soft tissue sarcomas (77.5% accuracy)”, “Palliative care and end of life “(71.9% accuracy), and “Other topics”(81.8% accuracy). The least accuracy of ChatGPT 4o were in “Colorectal tumors” (52.7% accuracy) and “Genitourinary tumors” (56.3% accuracy). Llama-3 showed a generally lower performance, with accuracy rates ranging from 28.4% in “Colorectal tumors” to 64.4% in “Basic oncology, epidemiology, and statistics”. The model struggled particularly in categories like “Thoracic tumors” (29.3% accuracy) and “Head and neck tumors” (30.8% accuracy), highlighting areas where its capabilities were significantly limited. Gemini 1.5 demonstrated mixed results, with some categories showing relatively higher accuracy rates compared to Lllama-3. For example, in “Skin tumors”, Gemini 1.5 achieved an accuracy rate of 68.2%, outperforming both ChatGPT 4o and Llama-3 in this category. However, its overall performance was still below that of ChatGPT 4o, with an accuracy rate of 47.3% across all categories. The models were assessed on their ability to correctly answer questions within each category, with the number of questions, correct answers, and corresponding percentages provided in Table 3 . Based on the one-way repeated measures ANOVA results (F = 4.88, p < 0.001), there were significant differences in model performance across topics. In particular, the lowest performance was observed in colorectal tumors (mean = 48.3%, SD = 17.7%) and genitourinary tumors (mean = 50.8%, SD = 15.5%). Table 3. Model performance by topics Category Question counts Correct answers (%) Claude 3.5 ChatGPT 4o Llama-3 Gemini 1.5 Basic oncology, epidemiology and statistics 118 106 (89.8%) 102 (86.4%) 76 (64.4%) 71 (60.2%) Thoracic tumors 75 53 (70.7%) 47 (62.7%) 22 (29.3%) 36 (48%) Non-colorectal gastrointestinal tumors 74 58 (78.4%) 47 (63.5%) 26 (35.1%) 33 (44.6%) Colorectal tumors 74 52 (70.3%) 39 (52.7%) 21 (28.4%) 31 (41.9%) Breast tumors 73 56 (76.7%) 46 (63%) 28 (38.4%) 32 (43.8%) Genitourinary tumors 64 45 (70.3%) 36 (56.3%) 24 (37.5%) 25 (39.1%) Gynecological tumors 57 40 (70.2%) 38 (66.7%) 24 (42.1%) 22 (38.6%) Palliative care and end of life 57 44 (77.2%) 41 (71.9%) 24 (42.1%) 27 (47.4%) Hematological tumors 40 31 (77.5%) 27 (67.5%) 15 (37.5%) 19 (47.5%) Bone tumors and soft tissue sarcomas 40 31 (77.5%) 31 (77.5%) 16 (40%) 15 (37.5%) Head and neck tumors 39 28 (71.8%) 24 (61.5%) 12 (30.8%) 16 (41%) Other topics 33 26 (78.8%) 27 (81.8%) 13 (39.4%) 20 (60.6%) Central nervous system tumors 24 17 (70.8%) 18 (75%) 12 (50%) 12 (50%) Skin tumors 22 18 (81.8%) 14 (63.6%) 6 (27.3%) 15 (68.2%) Total 790 605 (76.6%) 537 (68%) 319 (40.4%) 374 (47.3%) Open in a new tab The graphical presentations of the question topics, overall and category-specific performances of the models are presented in Fig. 1 . Fig. 1. Open in a new tab A : Model performance over eight annual board exams from 2016 to 2024. Claude 3.5 consistently scored the highest, followed by ChatGPT 4o, while Llama-3 and Gemini 1.5 showed lower and more variable performance. B : Models’ accuracy across different oncology topics. Claude 3.5 and ChatGPT 4o demonstrate the highest performance in most topics, with Claude 3.5 leading in “Basic oncology” and “Skin tumors.” C : Number of exams passed by each model out of the eight administered. Claude 3.5 passed all 8 exams, ChatGPT 4o passed 7, Llama-3 passed 4, and Gemini 1.5 passed 3. D : Overall scores (mean performance across all years and topics) for each model. Claude 3.5 achieved the highest overall score, followed by ChatGPT 4o, while Llama-3 and Gemini 1.5 lagged significantly behind Discussion Our study demonstrates that large language models (LLMs) have varying levels of proficiency in oncological knowledge when assessed using standardized board examination questions. Claude 3.5 Sonnet and ChatGPT 4o significantly outperformed Llama-3 and Gemini 1.5, suggesting that the architecture, training data, and model size play crucial roles in their medical knowledge base. The superior performance of Claude 3.5 Sonnet and ChatGPT 4o may be attributed to more extensive training datasets and advanced algorithms that better capture complex medical information. Previous studies have shown that larger models with more parameters tend to perform better on complex tasks, including medical question answering [ 1 , 9 , 10 ]. Their high accuracy in “Basic oncology, epidemiology, and statistics” indicates strong foundational knowledge, essential for clinical applications [ 11 ]. However, the decline in performance in recent years, particularly noticeable in the 2024 exam, suggests that these models may not be up to date with the most recent advancements in oncology. This is a known limitation of LLMs, as their training data often have a cutoff date, beyond which they do not incorporate new information. This highlights the importance of regularly updating LLMs with the latest medical literature and guidelines to maintain their relevance and accuracy [ 10 , 12 ]. The variability in performance across different oncology subspecialties indicates that while LLMs have a solid grasp of general oncology concepts, they may lack depth in specific areas such as “Genitourinary tumors” and “Colorectal tumors.” This could be due to less representation of these topics in their training data or the inherent complexity of these subjects. Prior research has indicated that LLMs may struggle with highly specialized or nuanced medical information that requires up-to-date knowledge and context. Llama-3 and Gemini 1.5 showed lower overall performance, with significant struggles in most categories. This suggests that smaller models or those with less specialized training data may not yet be suitable for applications requiring detailed medical knowledge. Enhancing their training data with more specialized oncology literature and improving their algorithms could bolster their capabilities [ 13 ]. Our findings align with previous studies evaluating the performance of LLMs in medical contexts. For instance, Kung et al. demonstrated that earlier versions of ChatGPT showed potential in medical education by performing well on the United States Medical Licensing Examination (USMLE) questions [ 14 ]. However, they also noted limitations in the models’ ability to handle complex clinical reasoning and the need for continuous updates to the training data. The promising results from Claude 3.5 Sonnet and ChatGPT 4o indicate that LLMs could become valuable tools in oncology education and clinical decision support. They have the potential to assist clinicians in staying updated with medical knowledge, support patient education, and enhance decision-making processes. However, caution is warranted due to inconsistencies and potential gaps in knowledge. The models should not be used as a sole source of information, and their outputs should be verified by medical professionals. This cautious approach is particularly important given that a survey of Turkish oncologists by Sahin revealed lower-than-expected AI literacy and utilization in clinical practice [ 15 ]. Limitations Our study has several limitations. The assessment was based on multiple-choice questions, which may not fully capture the complexity of clinical reasoning required in oncology practice. Additionally, the models were evaluated using Turkish-language questions. While all models confirmed their ability to understand Turkish, their proficiency in languages other than English may vary, potentially affecting performance. It is thought that LLMs perform better in English due to the abundance of English-language data in their training sets, and the models are often optimized for prompts and responses in English, which may affect the results of our study . Moreover, the LLMs’ knowledge cutoff dates may have impacted their ability to answer questions related to the most recent advancements in oncology. For instance, the decline in performance in the 2024 exam suggests that the models may not have access to the latest information, emphasizing the need for mechanisms to update LLMs with current medical knowledge. Conclusion This study provides valuable insights into the current capabilities and limitations of LLMs in the field of oncology. Significant differences in performance were observed among the four models, with Claude 3.5 Sonnet and ChatGPT 4o demonstrating superior oncological knowledge. The variability across topics and years underscores the need for ongoing development to improve their consistency and reliability. While LLMs show promise as tools for oncology education and decision support, they require regular updates and enhancements to ensure they can reliably support oncology practice in the future. Electronic supplementary material Below is the link to the electronic supplementary material. Supplementary Material 1 (93.3KB, xlsx) Acknowledgements As the authors, we would like to thank Dr. Nuri Karadurmuş, the president of Turkish Society of Medical Oncology and the Turkish Society of Medical Oncology for their unconditional support. Abbreviations AI Artificial Intelligence ANOVA Analysis of Variance LLM Large Language Models SD Standard Deviation USMLE United States Medical Licensing Examination Author contributions E.C.E: Writing, Data Curation, Formal Analysis E.E.K: Editing, Data Curation, Conceptalizuation. Funding The study was not funded by any organization or corporation. Data availability The datasets generated are available in appendix. Declarations Ethics approval and consent to participate Ethical approval was not required for this study as it involved the analysis of publicly available examination questions and did not include any human participants or personal data. Permission to use the examination questions was obtained from the Turkish Society of Medical Oncology. Consent for publication Not applicable. Competing interests The authors declare no competing interests. Footnotes Publisher’s note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. Shen Y, Heacock L, Elias J, Hentel KD, Reig B, Shih G, Moy L. ChatGPT and other large Language models are double-edged swords. Radiology. 2023;307. 10.1148/radiol.230163. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] 2. Liu M, Okuhara T, Chang X, Shirabe R, Nishiie Y, Okada H, Kiuchi T. Performance of ChatGPT Across different versions in Medical Licensing examinations Worldwide: systematic review and Meta-analysis. J Med Internet Res. 2024;26:e60807. 10.2196/60807. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Ali SR, Dobbs TD, Hutchings HA, Whitaker IS. Using ChatGPT to write patient clinic letters. Lancet Digit Health. 2023;5:e179–81. 10.1016/S2589-7500(23)00048-1. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Meropol NJ, Schrag D, Smith TJ, Mulvey TM, Langdon RM, Blum D, Ubel PA, Schnipper LE. American Society of Clinical Oncology Guidance Statement: the cost of Cancer Care. J Clin Oncol. 2009;27:3868–74. 10.1200/JCO.2009.23.1183. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Krittanawong C, Johnson KW, Rosenson RS, Wang Z, Aydar M, Baber U, Min JK, Tang WHW, Halperin JL, Narayan SM. Deep learning for cardiovascular medicine: a practical primer. Eur Heart J. 2019;40:2058–73. 10.1093/eurheartj/ehz056. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Esteva A, Kuprel B, Novoa RA, Ko J, Swetter SM, Blau HM, Thrun S. Dermatologist-level classification of skin cancer with deep neural networks. Nature. 2017;542:115–8. 10.1038/nature21056. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Erdat EC, Yalciner M, Urun Y. 