lopathy radioresistant bilsky deacetylase chemosensitive overrepresent dissimilarities banked csci bollen dijkstra rhma esperance gaudreault oncologie cepo e478 e490 wewel nater sahgal fehlings loblaw laperriere mackillop barzilai boriani 98s 107s ashamalla macedon intracavitary cement samarium vics berenson pflugmacher jarzem kyphoplasty clausen frevert lonn e401 onen simsek naderi bludau welzel kyphoplasty detsky versteeg sahgal eposo abdelhameid saro fadl posterolateral corpectomy pyramesh dorsolumbar cyw transpedicular morgen karbo svardal stelmer gehrchen spines tatsui amini s73 s82 fehlings nater tetreault aospine kamnerdsupaphon cyberknife intradural extramedullary capp trog superdex kamnerdsupaphon kieran onar farnesyltransferase lonafarnib kretschmar krailo couluris freyer krischer cyproheptadine periactin megestrol megace amdur synold mamelak topotecan topotecan fiveash isotoxicity s278 ahern vorinostat qaddoumi kocak panandiker rief omlor gururangan fangusaro poussaint gerszten kieran cediranib azd2171 bioavailable vegfr rood ptc299 pbtc chintagumpala eckel krailo pichon campion delpon noncompressive zoledronate jakacki acns0423 gnekow kandels dunne icorg guckenberger versteeg velden myrehaug hoskin multifraction scorad thirion dunne sciubba lapatinib supratentorial infratentorial osteocool opus rades segedin rades segedin aro rief heinhold mscc gning brinjikji kallmes vertebroplasty kyphoplasty neurointerv sahgal whyne fehlings cement hqo vertebroplasty kyphoplasty hqontario tsoumakidou cirse laufer sahgal s246 s253 dabravolski eßer lahm merk coblation prologo buethe mortell coblation gokaslan s124 s130 patchell tibbs regine decompressive fehlings nater holmer s99 s105 furlan sandoval nosyk foote deverall nater tetreault kopjar nater e613 glicksman tjong wfp soltys yahanda buchowski wegner patchell califf samartzis alini galbusera casaroli bassani jor e1044 massaad hadzipasic breckenridge neurospine e809 e817 wahab','Abstract The field of spinal oncology has substantially evolved over the past decades. This review synthesizes and appraises what was learned and what will potentially be discovered from the recently completed and ongoing clinical studies related to the treatment of primary and secondary spinal neoplasms. This scoping review included all clinical studies on the treatment of spinal neoplasms registered in the ClinicalTrials.gov website from February 2000 to December 2020. The terms “spinal cord tumor,” “spinal metastasis,” and “metastatic spinal cord compression” were used. Of the 174 registered clinical studies on primary spinal tumors and spinal metastasis, most of the clinical studies registered in this American registry were interventional studies led by single institutions in North America (n = 101), Europe (n = 43), Asia (n = 24), or other continents (n = 6). The registered clinical studies mainly focused on treatment strategies for spinal neoplasms (90.2%) that included investigating stereotactic radiosurgery (n = 33), radiotherapy (n = 21), chemotherapy (n = 20), and surgical technique (n = 11). Of the 69 completed studies, the results from 44 studies were published in the literature. In conclusion, this review highlights the key features of the 174 clinical studies on spinal neoplasms that were registered from 2000 to 2020. Clinical trials were heavily skewed toward the metastatic population as opposed to the primary tumors which likely reflects the rarity of the latter condition and associated challenges in undertaking prospective clinical studies in this population. This review serves to emphasize the need for a focused approach to enhancing translational research in spinal neoplasms with a particular emphasis on primary tumors. Keywords: chemotherapy, clinical trials, primary spinal tumors, radiotherapy, spinal metastases, surgical treatment The spinal column is a common site of metastatic cancer, whereas primary spinal neoplasms are relatively rare. The latter has estimated incidence rates of 0.74 to 2.5 cases per 100 000 population yearly in the literature, and primary spinal neoplasms account for 5% to 12% of all primary CNS neoplasms. 1 Metastatic neoplasms to the spinal column occur in 3% to 5% of all patients with cancer, and there has been an escalation in the incidence of spinal metastasis due to the increasing overall cancer survival and aging population. 2 Primary spinal neoplasms can rise from the intramedullary and extramedullary spinal tissues including the spinal cord, meninges, nerves, fat, bone, and blood vessels. 1 They most commonly originate from extramedullary structures (88% to 90% of the cases) including intradural (60%) or extradural compartments (25%), or cauda equina (15%) in adults. 1 The most common extramedullary tumors in adults are schwannomas (29%), meningiomas (25%), exophytic ependymomas (13%) and sarcomas (12%). 1 In contrast, the majority of the intramedullary tumors are either ependymomas (55%) or astrocytomas (30%) in adults. 1 The spine is the most common site for seeding of metastatic cancer occurring in 70% of patients with advanced solid tumors from breast, prostate, and lung. 3 , 4 The thoracic spine is the most frequent location for spinal metastasis (60% to 80%), followed by the lumbosacral spine (15% to 30%) and cervical spine (<10%). 4 Spinal metastatic neoplasms typically cause pain which is also the most common symptom at presentation (80% to 95%), but also can result in vertebral collapse and metastatic spinal cord compression (MESCC). 4 , 5 The latter is considered an oncologic emergency that can occur in 5% to 10% of patients with cancer, and it requires prompt diagnosis and immediate treatment. 3 In a population-based study in the province of Ontario, Canada, the estimated frequency of metastatic spinal cord compression was 0.23% at the time of diagnosis, accounting for 17 000 new cases of MESCC in the United States annually. 6 Over the past 3 decades, there has been a substantial improvement in the treatment protocols for surgical resection and adjuvant therapies for primary spinal tumors and spinal metastases. 5 , 7 The advances in diagnosis and treatment of patients with spinal neoplasm have been leveraged by an increasing scientific interest in studying more innovative therapies in the field of spinal oncology. This scoping review was performed to synthesize and appraise what was learned and what will potentially be discovered in the near future from the recently completed and ongoing clinical studies on the treatment of individuals with spinal neoplasms that were registered in the ClinicalTrials.gov website since its inception. Methods This scoping review followed the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA) guidelines. This review included all clinical studies on the treatment of primary and secondary spinal neoplasms registered in the ClinicalTrials.gov website from February 2000 to December 30, 2020. The terms “spinal cord tumors,” “spinal metastasis,” and “metastatic spinal cord compression” were used. The clinical studies were classified as per the “study type” into interventional and observational, according to the data registered by the investigators. Based on their focus, the clinical studies were then grouped into diagnosis, prevention, prognosis, registry, and treatment. Adolescents were defined as individuals with ages between 12 and 17 years, and adults constituted those individuals with 18 years of age or older at the time of study recruitment. The results of the completed clinical studies were synthetized and appraised. A summary of the search results was reported using the newly developed version of the PRISMA flow chart for systematic reviews that included searches of databases and registers only. 8 All available publications were obtained from the links provided in the Clinicaltrials.gov website if available; otherwise, the results were retrieved from the PubMed website using the name of the principal investigator to identify the publications. ClinicalTrials.gov Website ClinicalTrials.gov is an American Web-based resource that is publicly available to anyone who wants to search for information of clinical studies on many diseases and conditions including spinal neoplasms. 9 The website was developed by the National Institutes of Health, through the National Library of Medicine, that has been overseeing the website’s maintenance since its inception in February 2000. 9 The sponsor, or principal investigator, of the clinical study is responsible for inputting new study information and updating the study information. Most of the studies registered in the website are typically led by an institution located in the United States, even though the website registered studies from 193 other countries to date. 9 While the majority of the registered studies are clinical trials where human volunteers are assigned to interventions (ie, “interventional studies”), there are also observational studies on investigational drugs outside of clinical trials (expanded access). 9 Results Of the 225 registered studies on management of spinal neoplasms captured using the above search strategy, 46 studies were duplicated and 6 were excluded because they included participants with brain neoplasms only (n = 3) or peripheral nerve neoplasms (n = 1), or it was focused on postoperative pain control without particularly targeting spinal neoplasms (n = 1) ( Figure 1 ). Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA) flow diagram using the newly developed version for new systematic reviews which included searches of databases and registers only (* indicates the number of records marked as ineligible using an automation tool; ** indicates the number of reports of included studies that were published in the literature). 8 Of the 174 studies on spinal neoplasms captured using the above search strategy, most of them were interventional (78.7%) and single-institutional studies (71.3%) ( Table 1 ). There has been an average of 8 clinical studies registered a year since 2000. Most of the clinical studies were led by an institution located in the United States (n = 92) followed by Germany (n = 14), China (n = 11), France (n = 10), Canada (n = 9), and Taiwan (n = 8); the Netherlands (n = 4), Denmark, Egypt, South Korea, and Switzerland (n = 3 each); Australia, Ireland, Italy, and United Kingdom (n = 2 each); and Belgium, Finland, Israel, Malaysia, Singapore, and Turkey (n = 1 each). By December 2020, most of the registered clinical studies were reported as “completed” (39.1%), “recruiting” (21.8%), “unknown status” (12.6%), or “active, not recruiting” (11.5%) ( Table 1 ). While the majority of the registered clinical studies did not specify the phase at which the study was designed for (55.2%), there were 32 phase-2 studies, 23 phase-1 studies, 7 phase-3 studies, and 3 phase-4 studies ( Table 1 ). The vast majority of those registered clinical trials included participants of both sexes (98.8%) in adulthood (49.4%), and a single study enrolled only males with metastatic prostate cancer ( NCT01219790 ) while a second study included only females with metastatic breast cancer ( NCT03365973 ) ( Figure 2 ). Table 1. Characteristic of the Registered Clinical Studies on Management of Patients With Spinal Neoplasms That Were Registered in the ClinicalTrials.gov Website From February 2000 to December 2020 (n = 174) Categories Features N (%) Type of study Interventional studies 137 (78.7%) Observational studies 37 (21.3%) Type of sponsor Single-institution studies 124 (71.3%) Multicenter studies 50 (28.7%) Current status “Completed” 69 (39.1%) “Recruiting” 38 (21.8%) “Unknown status” 22 (12.6%) “Active, not recruiting” 20 (11.5%) “Terminated” 19 (10.9%) “Withdrawn” a 5 (2.9%) “Suspended” b 1 (0.6%) Study phase Not available or not applicable 96 (55.2%) Phase 1 (studies primarily focused on safety analysis) 23 (13.2%) Phase 2 (studies on efficacy and side effects) 32 (18.4%) Phase 1/2 10 (5.7%) Phase 3 (studies on efficacy, effectiveness, and safety) 7 (4.0%) Phase 2/3 3 (1.7%) Phase 4 (postmarketing surveillance studies) 3 (1.7%) Type of neoplasm Spinal metastasis 94 (54.0%) Primary spinal tumors 47 (27.0%) Primary or metastatic spinal neoplasms 37 (19.0%) Open in a new tab a Studies were “terminated” due to challenges in recruiting participants (n = 9) or funding support (n = 4, being withdrawal of funds from industry in 3), device under study was approved by the Food and Drug Administration (n = 2), another similar treatment became clinically available (n = 1), sufficient data collection for a qualitative analysis was achieved (n = 1), principal investigator moved from the leading institution (n = 1), or there was an undisclosed reason (n = 1). b Studies were “withdrawn” due to treatment toxicity, sponsor closed the study, the study protocol needed to be redesigned, “feasibility issues”, or lack of collaborating centers. Fig. 2. Open in a new tab Age distribution of the participants who were recruited for the clinical studies on spinal neoplasms that were registered in the ClinicalTrials.gov website from February 2000 to December 2020 (* identifies studies that included only children). While most of clinical studies were specific to spinal metastasis (n = 94), 47 studies included primary spinal tumors and 37 included individuals with either primary or metastatic spinal neoplasms ( Table 1 ). Most of the registered clinical studies were focused on treatment strategies for spinal neoplasms (n = 157), whereas fewer clinical studies either created registries (n = 6) or addressed questions on diagnosis (n = 4), prognosis (n = 4), and prevention (n = 3) in the field of spinal oncology. There was a diversity of topics on the management of spinal neoplasms with the majority investigating stereotactic radiosurgery (n = 33), radiotherapy (n = 21), chemotherapy (n = 20), or surgical technique and instrumentation (n = 11) ( Figure 3 ). Of the 71 “completed” clinical investigations, 35 studies did not have their results posted in the website but were found in the peer-reviewed literature, 11 studies had their results posted and published, 22 studies did not have their results posted or published, and 3 studies had their results posted in the website but not published in the literature. From the 44 published studies, their main findings were grouped into (i) surgical treatment, 10–23 and (ii) nonsurgical interventions 23–55 including validation of a novel assessment tool for patients with spinal neoplasms 56 ( Tables 2 and 3 , respectively). Fig. 3. Open in a new tab Distribution of all the clinical studies registered in the ClinicalTrials.gov website from February 2000 to December 2020 (n = 174) that were grouped into treatment (black bars), registry (light gray bars), prognosis (dark gray bars), prevention (white bars), and diagnosis (stripped bars). Table 2. Summary of the Published Findings From the Registered Clinical Studies on Surgical Treatment of Patients With Spinal Neoplasms Year NCT number Main findings Type Reference 2009 NCT00374751 Intravertebral administration of 153 Sm combined with kyphoplasty is tolerated and provides adequate pain control in patients with spinal metastases. There were no hematologic adverse effects. A reduction of the bone scan tracer uptake was detected in the injected vertebrae. Spinal metastases 10 2011 NCT00211237 Kyphoplasty is an effective and safe treatment option for pain control and functional recovery in patients with cancer and vertebral compression fractures. Spinal metastases 11 2015 NCT01365715 Preoperative transcatheter arterial embolization of spinal metastases did not significantly diminish intraoperative blood loss and allogeneic red blood cell transfusion but did decrease the surgery duration in patients with symptomatic metastatic spinal cord compression. A small reduction of intraoperative blood loss was s