f motor impairment, ambulation, pain control, and 6-month survival rates. Spinal metastases 53 , 54 2017 NCT00276640 The addition of etoposide to vincristine carboplatin did not improve 5-year progression-free survival or overall survival of children with newly diagnosed low-grade glioma. High nonprogression rates at 24 weeks justify the use of vincristine carboplatin as the first-line therapy. Primary spinal neoplasms 43 2018 NCT00974168 Primary radiotherapy significantly improves quality of life (using EORTC QLQ–C30) in patients with malignant spinal cord compression who do not undergo surgical decompression. There was no significant difference between a 10-Gy single-fraction radiation schedule and one with 20 Gy in 5 daily fractions on posttreatment EORTC QLQ–C30 Summary scores. Spinal metastases 44 2018 NCT01594892 Dose-intensified hypofractionated SBRT resulted in durable local metastasis control, marked and long-term pain responses, and improved quality of life in patients with painful spinal metastases. Spinal metastases 45 2018 NCT02622841 SBRT followed by surgical stabilization within 24 h for the treatment of patients with symptomatic unstable spinal metastases is feasible and safe. The combination of both treatments within 24 h decreases the treatment burden for the patient, and may result in earlier and better pain control and local control rates than current standard of care of surgery followed by external beam radiotherapy. Spinal metastases 46 2018 NCT02512965 24 Gy in 2 SBRT daily fractions is safe and effective in achieving high tumor control rates for de novo spinal metastases. Spinal metastases 47 2019 NCT00727584 Overall, a single dose of radiotherapy, compared with a multifraction dose delivered over 5 days, did not meet the criterion for noninferiority for the primary outcome (ambulatory at 8 weeks) in patients with malignant metastatic solid tumors and spinal canal compression. Spinal metastases 48 2020 NCT00968643 Single 10-Gy fraction is noninferior to 20 Gy in 5 fractions to preserve mobility in patients with malignant spinal cord compression who do not undergo surgical decompression. Spinal metastases 49 2020 NCT03249584 Radiofrequency ablation is safe and effective providing rapid (within 3 days) and significant pain improvement with sustained long-term relief through 6 months in patients treated for metastatic bone disease. Spinal metastases 52 Open in a new tab Abbreviations: vAUC, Area Under the Curve; CSF, cerebrospinal fluid; EORTC, European Organisation for Research and Treatment of Cancer. Discussion The results of this scoping review highlight the key features of the 174 clinical studies on primary spinal tumors and spinal metastasis that were registered in the ClinicalTrials.gov website from 2000 to 2020. The vast majority of the clinical studies registered in this American registry were interventional studies led by single institutions located in North America (n = 101) followed by Europe (n = 43), Asia (n = 24), or another continent (n = 6). The registered clinical studies were mostly focused on treatment strategies for spinal neoplasms (90.2%) with stereotactic radiosurgery (n = 33), radiotherapy (n = 21), chemotherapy (n = 20), and surgical intervention (n = 11) as the top topics among many other new therapies. There were also few clinical studies on registries (3.4%), diagnosis (2.3%), prognosis (2.3%), and prevention (1.7%). Of the 71 completed clinical studies, the results from 44 studies were published in the peer-reviewed literature including advances in surgical treatment (n = 13), nonsurgical interventions such as new radiotherapic techniques and chemotherapies (n = 30), or validation of a novel assessment tool in the field of spinal oncology (n = 1). Surgical Interventions This review captured 42 registered surgical studies focused on preoperative evaluation, use of embolization, surgical techniques, combined or comparative therapies involving spine surgery, and postoperative management of individuals with spinal neoplasm. In addition to exploring novel minimally invasive surgical procedures, many registered surgical studies have been investigating the use of new surgical techniques such as surgical navigating systems, bone graft substitutes, spine fusion systems, and intraoperative monitoring among many others. To date, 13 registered surgical studies have been completed with final results published. 10–22 , 52 Overall, the most commonly studied and published topic was on the feasibility, safety, and effectiveness of vertebral augmentation in the management of pathologic vertebral compression fractures. Vertebroplasty is a form of vertebral augmentation where bone cement is injected into the fractured vertebral body in order to stabilize the spine and provide pain relief. Kyphoplasty is a modified technique of vertebroplasty in which a small balloon initially creates within the fractured vertebral body where the cement is eventually injected. 57 The safety and effectiveness of this technique in the management of painful vertebral compression fractures in patients with metastatic spine cancer was demonstrated in Cancer Patient Fracture Evaluation (CAFE) study reported by Berenson et al. 11 There remains controversy whether one technique is better than the other and a prior systematic review suggested that both the short- and long-term outcomes on pain and degree of disability in patients who underwent vertebroplasty were similar to those who underwent kyphoplasty. 57 Although both vertebral augmentation techniques are considered relatively safe for select groups of patients with spinal bone metastases, there are rare but serious complications of those procedures such as cement extravasation, new fractures at adjacent levels, and “tumor extravasation” causing an unusual rapid tumor spread pattern into the adjacent tissues. 58 In a cost-utility analysis in Ontario (Canada), the use of kyphoplasty or vertebroplasty in the management of pathologic vertebral compression fractures in patients with cancer was considered to be potentially cost-effective, even though a more widespread use of vertebral augmentation would eventually increase the overall net health care costs. 59 When compared with nonsurgical treatment, kyphoplasty and vertebroplasty were associated with an incremental cost-effectiveness ratio of CAD$33 471 and CAD$17 870 (in 2016) per quality-adjusted life-year gained, respectively. Given its potential benefits and risks of adverse effects, the Cardiovascular and Interventional Radiological Society of Europe (CIRSE) has recently issued their guidelines for the indications, contraindications, safety profile, and outcomes of the percutaneous vertebral augmentation. 60 As technology continues to advance, other surgical adjuncts have been also developed and tested such as the use of spinal laser interstitial thermotherapy for management of metastatic spinal epidural compression, and the use of radiofrequency and photodynamic techniques for ablation of spinal metastases. 14 , 21 , 52 Moreover, vertebroplasty or kyphoplasty has been used in combination with other minimally invasive treatment strategies in the management of symptomatic spinal metastases. Kyphoplasty combined with intraoperative radiotherapy may also enhance pain relief and progression-free survival particularly in adults older than 50 years of age with painful vertebral metastases. 14 Similarly, photodynamic therapy has been shown to be feasible and safe tumor-ablative modality that can be delivered in conjunction with vertebroplasty or balloon kyphoplasty in patients with symptomatic spinal metastases. 15 There is also emerging evidence to support the use of minimally invasive techniques to resect metastatic tumors and to stabilize the spinal column. 61 Recent advances have incorporated microsurgical approaches, image guidance, and percutaneous screw fixation techniques. 61 These approaches are particularly attractive in the medically frail patient with spinal instability or in cases where “separation surgery” to achieve epidural clearance to enable stereotactic body radiotherapy (SBRT) is appropriate. One of the minimally invasive options which is under active investigation for metastatic spinal tumors is the use of percutaneous, image-guided coblation to use thermal energy to safely ablate neoplastic tissue. 62 Coblation techniques are appealing when combined with percutaneous cement augmentation of vertebra bodies compromised by cancer. 63 Open invasive surgery remains the treatment of choice for selected groups of patients who developed spinal metastases associated with mechanical instability and high-grade spinal cord compression where surgical treatment can improve the patient’s quality of life and functional status. 7 , 22 , 64 In this context, it is noteworthy to highlight a landmark clinical trial by Patchell et al. that demonstrated the superiority of the direct decompressive surgery followed by postoperative radiotherapy over radiotherapy alone in the management of patients with spinal cord compression due to metastatic cancer. 65 Despite the paucity of the economic analyses in this field, a prior systematic review identified 2 cost-effectiveness studies in which their results suggest that surgery followed by radiotherapy is costlier but clinically more effective than radiotherapy alone for the management of patients with MESCC. 66–68 Despite substantial advancements in nonsurgical treatments as stressed herein, surgery also remains the mainstay treatment in most cases of primary spinal tumor. 64 In any case, treatment planning ought to rely on a multidisciplinary team approach that accounts for the patient’s clinical and neurological assessment associated with histopathological diagnosis, and local and systemic staging of the neoplastic disease. 7 , 64 , 69 An area of active investigation relates to the use of clinical prediction tools to facilitate a personalized medicine approach to the management of patients with metastatic spinal tumors. 70 , 71 It is recognized that baseline performance status, metastatic burden, the time of primary tumor, and the presence/absence of medical frailty are key factors in decision-making. 70 , 71 It is anticipated that future studies incorporating molecular subtyping of tumors and biomarker-based approaches to assess patient heterogeneity will have an important impact on clinical decision-making. 70 , 71 Altogether, the results of this review emphasize several research initiatives on less invasive surgical techniques in selected cases of spinal neoplasm, whereas many other registered and ongoing surgical studies are focused on the refinement of the open procedures or surgical treatment of patients with spinal neoplasms. Primary and metastatic spinal neoplams are complex and challenging diseases to be treated and, while many advances are still under investigation, spine surgeons and other treating physicians are always encouraged to follow the evidence-based oncological principles. 7 , 64 Nonsurgical Therapies Most of the registered clinical studies in this review (n = 109) are focused on a variety of nonsurgical therapies in the management of patients with a spinal neoplasm, including stereotactic radiosurgery also referred to as SBRT, new chemotherapic drugs, modified radiotherapic techniques, photodynamic therapy, photon therapy, pharmacological treatments, and isometric resistance training. Until now, 31 registered clinical studies were completed and their final results published. 23–51 , 53–55 Over the past 2 decades, SBRT has been the most frequently studied nonsurgical treatment among the studies that were registered in the ClinicalTrials.gov website. In a recent review, the results of 59 studies including 5655 patients suggest that SBRT typically results in high rates of local tumor control with moderate rates of adverse effects in a selected group of patients with spinal metastases. 72 Only recently that 2 major phase-3 randomized controlled trials have been conducted and reported in abstract form. RTOG 0631 ( NCT00922974 ) and the Canadian SC-24 study ( NCT 02512965 ) have discordant results despite the fact that both evaluate the complete response rate at 3 months as compared to conventional palliative radiotherapy. There are distinct design and inclusion criteria differences between the 2 trials. However, the Canadian SC-24 study evaluating 24 Gy in 2 SBRT fractions to 20 Gy in 5 conventional radiotherapy fractions has concluded superior rates of complete response to pain at the treated study vertebral segment at 3 and 6 months after treatment, and represents a major therapeutic advance in symptom control for eligible patients with spinal metastases. Although SBRT has transformed the field of spinal oncology for metastatic disease and slowly for some primary extramedullary neoplasms, this therapy conveys a risk for adverse effects including pain flare (23% to 68%), vertebral compression fractures (5% to 42%), and radiation myelopathy (less than 1% when existing guidelines, advanced delivery techniques, and established safe dose schedules are followed). 7 , 72 These phase-3 trials represent de novo spinal metastases and an area of further evaluation lies in the postoperative management for spinal metastases. This typically involves adjuvant conventional radiation; however, postoperative spine SBRT is increasingly practiced with single-institution phase-2 data reported, and without phase-3 evidence, consensus guidelines for postoperative SBRT was established by an international group of experts. 73 Their “consensus treatment indications included: radioresistant primary, 1 or 2 levels of adjacent disease, and previous radiation therapy. Contraindications included: involvement of more than 3 contiguous vertebral bodies, ASIA Grade A status (complete spinal cord injury without preservation of motor or sensory function), and postoperative Bilsky Grade 3 residual (cord compression without any CSF around the cord).” 73 Many other nonsurgical modalities of treatment for spinal neoplasms have been studied including modified regimens of external beam radiotherapy, new chemotherapeutic drugs, and other pharmacological therapies (eg, angiogenesis inhibitors, bisphosphonates, histone deacetylase inhibitor). Chemotherapy is typically delivered following definitive therapy with either radiotherapy or surgery followed by radiotherapy for the spinal metastases requiring treatment. The intent of systemic therapy is overall disease control as opposed to spinal specific outcomes. 74 However, chemotherapy alone can be applied in cases of metastases for treatment of markedly chemosensitive tumors such as lymphoma, seminoma, or neuroblastoma. 74 The use of chemotherapy in primary spinal tumors is presently restricted to few specific cases, even though new chemotherapies for low- or high-grade gliomas and recurrent ependymomas have been recently investigated with promising prospects. 36 , 38 , 42 , 43 , 51 , 64 By and large, the proper management of both primary spinal tumors and spinal metastases ought to rely on a multidisciplinary approach where surgical interventions and alternative or adjuvant nonsurgical therapies should be considered in every case. Evidence-based spinal oncology and the combined expertise of spine surgeons, neurologists, radiation oncologists, medical oncologists, and interventional radiologists remains the backbone of the management of patients with either a primary spinal tumor or a spinal metastasis. Similar to the oncological practice, collaborative work in research in this field is fundamental to explore and determine the most appropriate therapeutic choices for each biological type of tumor personalized to the patient’s characteristics, preferences, and expectations. Study Limitations While this scoping review included up-to-date information on clinical research on spinal oncology, there are some limitations that should be considered when interpreting and applying those results. First, this review was based exclusively on information from the ClinicalTrials.gov website and, therefore, the accuracy and completeness of the data depends upon the individuals who registered the studies. Prior investigators raised concerns about the quality assurance for all entries in this publicly available website long ago, which led to regulatory modifications to make data insertion more complete, timely, accurate, and explanatory. 75 , 76 Second, this scoping review using data from the ClinicalTrials.gov is basically a cross-sectional analysis of the recently completed or current/ongoing clinical studies at one point in time that may overrepresent interventional and observational studies in the field. For instance, there has been no registered study specifically exploring other innovative strategies such as the use of artificial intelligence and precision medicine in the field of spinal oncology, which have the potential to revolutionize the care of patients with spinal neoplasm. 71 , 77–79 Finally, the ClinicalTrials.gov website is an American initiative containing mostly registered studies from the United States followed by other non-Asian countries. This may represent a source of bias and may limit their generalizability. For instance, the distribution of the primary sites of the spinal metastases significantly varies among different populations. 80 Conclusions The results of this scoping review underline the trends and characteristics of the recently completed and ongoing clinical studies on the management of primary spinal tumors and spinal metastases. Most of those research initiatives have been interventional studies on SBRT, chemotherapy, and external radiotherapy, even though a variety of topics in spine oncology has been investigated over the past 2 decades. Since North American and European institutions lead most of the clinical studies in this field, their applicability in Asian, African, and Latin American countries may be limited due to the dissimilarities in the epidemiology of spinal neoplasms, health care access, and coverage among different populations. 1 , 80 Lastly, the relatively high number of clinical studies on spinal oncology that were “terminated,” “withdrawn,” or “suspended” (n = 25) underscores the challenges in clinical research in this field. In particular, the rarity and heterogeneity of the spinal neoplasms represent a major challenge in the recruitment of participants and the affordability of clinical studies in spinal oncology that commonly require long-term sustainability. 81 Given the relative rarity of primary spinal neoplasms, prospective high-quality registries, comparative effectiveness studies, and molecular investigations into banked neoplastic tissue are urgently required to allow for the examination of diagnosis, prognosis, and optimal treatment protocols. Acknowledgments Meeting presentation: An abstract with data included in this manuscript was accepted for presentation and received the Second Place Award in the Technology Innovation category in the 9th National Spinal Cord Injury Conference of the Canadian Spinal Cord Injury Rehabilitation Association (CSCI-RA) in November 16–18, 2021. Conflict of interest statement. The authors declare no conflict of interest related to this study. Authorship statement . J.C.F. contributed significantly to the study design and its implementation including collection, analysis, and interpretation of the data. J.R.W., E.M.M., A.S., and M.G.F. contributed significantly to the analysis and interpretation of the data. All authors reviewed and agreed with the final version of the manuscript. Funding This study was carried out without funding support. References 1. Newton HB. Overview of spinal cord tumor epidemiology. In: Newton HB, ed. Handbook of Neuro-Oncology Neuroimaging. 2nd ed. Academic Press, San Diego, CA, Cambridge, MA, London, UK and Oxford, UK: Elsevier; 2016:35–39. [