INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(292,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9058183','Protocol for a pilot randomized controlled trial of a mobile health exercise intervention for older patients with myeloid neoplasms (GO-EXCAP 2).','Loh KP, Sanapala C, Janelsins M, Klepin HD, Schnall R, Culakova E, Sohn MB, Vertino P, Susiarjo M, Jensen-Battaglia M, Becker MW, Liesveld J, Mendler JH, Huselton E, Lin PJ, Mustian K.','2021','cytosine cytosine ifny personalizing wilmot urmc wci r00ca237744 ug1ca189961 wci nct04981821 oncoloyg synced garmin vivofit pencil walked oars oars oars selfefficacy actigraphy bioelectrical rjl bioelectrical handgrip dynamometer isokinetic bts freeemg heartbeat firstbeat stnfr1 stnfr2 ifny sil sil ifny oars sil sil stnfr1 stnfr2 acsm preloaded booklet booklet enactment synced synced freezer stnfr1 stnfr2 ifny sil sil luminex dneasy nanodrop spectrophotometer bisulfite infinium interrogates dinucleotides minfi unmethylated sva acsm enactment inspect distributional hypomethylating mplus urmc wci dsmc dsmc dsmc dsmc dsmc dsmc fitter urmc wci ug1 ca189961 r00ca237744 kpl k24 ag056589 k24nr018621 wilmot kpl r33ag059206 energetics trec r25ca203650 arber orazi hasserjian statfacts amyl ramsdale culakova e10296 carp waldman 15_suppl 15_suppl nobile alimena hurria togawa mohile klepin tooze myelogenous sikorskii tesnjak victorson srkalovic harhay harhay tinsley thapa 17s s79 clml sajid mustian mustian janelsins peppone kamen guido heckler urcc ncorp 15_suppl 15_suppl vaniterson helenowski rademaker courneya cosponsored mohile urcc ncorp 15_suppl 15_suppl klepin danhauer tooze myelogenous alibhai smh schlombs leukres hentschel gobel leukres kleckner courneya moschny platen klaaßen trampisch hinrichs sjors trolle lagerros sjolander balter ijmr egerton chastin sfm stensvold helbostad glv150 okubo woodbury delbaere panneerselvam sonis dmk nfkappab mucotoxic lamkin lutgendorf lubaroff beltz asfaha dubeykovskiy weymann ctrs elsea kneiss tooze nicklas panju danesh minden kelvin alibhai smh brække norheim imgenberg kreuz jonsdottir kew008 monozygotic s41398 conneely becerra tsukui kanda nara nishino greiwe yarasheski semenkovich 0274com tapp saxton belshaw c46 ronn volkov davegardh e1003572 dietzmann mcb uddin koenen aiello wildman s0033291710001674 tapp saxton belshaw c46 radom aizik zaldivar tsukui kanda nara nishino greiwe yarasheski semenkovich 0274com gowers toth cyto campion milagro goyenechea mustian sprod janelsins 15_suppl 15_suppl kleckner kamen ncorp kleckner kamen gewandter sanapala feeley cossette champaign mustian peppone darling palesh heckler ctcaetm healthcaredelivery ostir volpato guralnik shuman paretsky belser holtzer beekman deeg limbeek braam tilburg jval aryee minfi bioconductor infinium btu049 leek storey sva bts034 bellg borrelli hartgers yazdanbakhsh houwing duistermaat defilippis s12859 woodman studenski pahor korn freidlin hunsberger ivy borm fransen lemmens wajg fairburn agras kiernan essex kupfer macarthur s101 s101 beran violato muthen muthen mplus muthen muthen mandrekar jto 0b013e3181e2eadf sanapala dinardo pullarkat nejmoa2012971 gutman purev bloodadvances','Abstract Introduction: We have shown the Exercise for Cancer Patients (EXCAP ©® ) exercise program improved physical function and symptoms and reduced inflammatory markers in patients with cancer. However, adherence to exercise was lower in older adults compared to their younger counterparts. We then leveraged a mobile app to deliver EXCAP ©® and adapted the intervention [Geriatric-Oncology (GO)-EXCAP] for older patients with myeloid neoplasms. In this pilot randomized trial, the primary goal is to determine effect sizes. We propose to assess the preliminary efficacy of GO-EXCAP compared to a behavioral placebo control on physical function, patient-reported outcomes (fatigue, mood, and quality of life), and inflammatory markers in 100 patients aged ≥60 years with myeloid neoplasms receiving outpatient chemotherapy. Methods: GO-EXCAP consists of the EXCAP ©® exercise prescription (daily home-based progressive aerobic walking and resistance exercises with rated perceived exercise of 5–8), EXCAP ©® kit (i.e., activity tracker, resistance bands, print manual, bag), a mobile app, and an in-person or virtual session with the exercise physiologist to deliver exercise prescription. The intervention will last for three cycles of chemotherapy (approximately 12 weeks). The primary outcome measure will be physical function (Short Physical Performance Battery). Secondary outcome measures include fatigue (Brief Fatigue Inventory), mood (Center for Epidemiologic Studies Depression Scale), and quality of life (Functional Assessment of Cancer Therapy-Leukemia). Exploratory outcome measures include inflammatory markers. Discussion: Older adults with myeloid neoplasms receiving outpatient chemotherapy serve as an ideal model for studying an individually tailored mobile health exercise intervention in vulnerable older patients receiving cancer treatments to prevent physical function decline and improve symptoms. Keywords: Mobile health, exercise intervention, geriatric hematology, myeloid neoplasms Introduction Myeloid neoplasms are a group of blood cancers. Common types include acute myeloid leukemia (AML), myelodysplastic syndrome (MDS), and MDS/myeloproliferative neoplasm overlap. 1 Myeloid neoplasms occur more frequently in adults aged ≥60 years. 2 Outpatient chemotherapy regimens are often preferred for this population and treatments usually continue until disease progression, death, or stem cell transplant. Over 70% of older patients with myeloid neoplasms receiving outpatient chemotherapy develop impairments in physical function and 33% report mood disturbances. 3 , 4 Physical function decline and symptoms (e.g., fatigue, mood disturbances) can lead to reduced quality of life (QoL), treatment interruptions, and reduced survival. 5 – 10 Therefore, it is imperative to develop interventions for older patients receiving outpatient chemotherapy that prevent physical function decline and improve symptoms so that they can maintain their QoL and continue to receive cancer treatments. Exercise may prevent physical function decline and improve symptoms. 11 – 14 Older adults can derive benefits from exercise even if they only maintain or slightly improve their physical activity levels. 14 Three pilot studies that evaluated exercise in older patients with myeloid neoplasms showed that they could exercise safely and that exercise might improve physical function, fatigue, mood, and QoL. 15 – 17 However, adherence to exercise in older adults is lower than their younger counterparts because they may face barriers such as lack of motivation, difficult with transportation, and limited access to exercise professionals. 18 – 22 Integrating a mobile health (mHealth) app with an exercise intervention has the potential to help older patients with myeloid neoplasms overcome these barriers, with the goal of preventing physical function decline and improving symptoms. 23 , 24 However, studies of mHealth exercise interventions tailored to this population are lacking. 24 A better understanding of the mechanisms of exercise-induced benefits could help identify biomarkers to tailor exercise prescriptions. Two potential mechanisms of interest are inflammation and epigenetics. Elevated pro-inflammatory markers such as tumor necrosis factor (TNF)α and other cytokines [e.g., interleukin-1 beta (IL-1β), and interleukin-6 (IL-6)] are associated with physical function decline, fatigue, mood disturbances, and worsening QoL. 25 – 31 DNA methylation, one of several epigenetic mechanisms, refers to the addition of a methyl group to cytosine residues in CpG sites (where a cytosine nucleotide is followed by a guanine). Studies have shown that genome-wide DNA methylation changes are also associated with inflammation as well as changes in physical function, fatigue, and mood. 32 – 34 Exercise has been shown to induce epigenetic changes and reduce inflammatory markers, which may in turn mitigate physical function decline and improve fatigue and mood. 35 – 39 Conceptual model Based on prior research and our research findings, 40 – 47 we propose a conceptual model as shown in Figure 1 . Aging, myeloid neoplasms, and outpatient chemotherapy induce changes in epigenetics and promote the production of pro-inflammatory cytokines such as TNFα and systemic inflammation, causing physical function decline, fatigue, mood disturbances, and worsening QoL. We hypothesize that exercise, in combination with a mobile app to enhance exercise self-efficacy and adherence, may induce epigenetic changes leading to decreased production of pro-inflammatory cytokines, which in turn may block aging- and/or chemotherapy-induced cytokine activation, and reduce inflammation and improve health outcomes. Figure 1: Open in a new tab Consent procedures for patients (in-person or verbal consent) We have previously shown the Exercise for Cancer Patients (EXCAP ©® ) exercise program improved physical function and symptoms and reduced inflammatory markers [(e.g., interferon gamma (IFNy), IL-1β] in patients with cancer. 12 , 18 , 48 – 50 However, adherence to exercise was lower in older adults compared to their younger counterparts. 18 We then leveraged a mobile app to deliver EXCAP ©® and adapted the intervention [Geriatric Oncology (GO)-EXCAP] for older patients with myeloid neoplasms in a qualitative study and single arm pilot study (GO-EXCAP 1). 51 In the qualitative study, older patients with myeloid neoplasms perceived the GO-EXCAP as being feasible, suggested ways to leverage a mobile app to deliver EXCAP ©® , and emphasized the importance of personalizing exercise goals. 51 The GO-EXCAP 1 single arm pilot study was recently completed and is being analyzed. In this pilot randomized trial (GO-EXCAP 2), we propose to assess the preliminary efficacy of the GO-EXCAP intervention compared to a behavioral placebo control on physical function, patient-reported outcomes, and inflammatory and epigenetic markers in older patients with myeloid neoplasms receiving outpatient chemotherapy. The primary goal of piloting is to determine effect sizes for use in a future large RCT, but we will also collect feasibility metrics and qualitative data to further optimize the study design, procedures, and intervention. 52 Aims and Hypothesis Our study aims: 1) To assess the preliminary efficacy of GO-EXCAP versus a behavioral placebo control on physical function [Short Physical Performance Battery (SPPB)]; 2) To assess the preliminary efficacy of GO-EXCAP versus a behavioral placebo control on fatigue, mood, and QoL; 3) To explore the effect of GO-EXCAP versus a behavioral placebo control on levels of inflammatory cytokines and genome-wide DNA methylation patterns. Methods Study Design, Setting, and Eligibility Criteria We will conduct a pilot randomized controlled trial of 100 patients. We will recruit patients from the University of Rochester Medical Center/Wilmot Cancer Institute (URMC/WCI) located in Rochester, NY, USA and its affiliated community practices. Eligibility criteria are shown in Table 1 . The protocol was approved in September 2021 by the University of Rochester Research Subjects Research Board. The study is funded by the National Cancer Institute (NCI; R00CA237744), with additional funding from the University of Rochester Cancer Center NCI Community Oncology Research Program (UG1CA189961), WCI, and University of Rochester Aging Institute. The clinical trial is registered on ClinicalTrials.gov ( NCT04981821 ). Table 1: Eligibility criteria Inclusion criteria Age ≥60 years (conventional definition of “older” in trials of myeloid neoplasms), A diagnosis of a myeloid neoplasm, Planned for or receiving outpatient chemotherapy, English speaking (because the GO-EXCAP intervention is currently available only in English), Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group Performance Status of 0–2, No medical contraindications for exercise per oncologist, Able to walk 4 meters as part of SPPB measured walk (with or without an assistive device) Able to provide informed consent Exclusion criterion Physical, psychological, or social impairments that would interfere with patient’s ability to participate in the study or participate in the intervention, as determined by the PI or the treating team Open in a new tab Abbreviations: GO-EXCAP, Geriatric Oncoloyg-Exercise for Cancer Patients; PI, Principal Investigator; SPPB, Short Physical Performance Battery Recruitment and Consent Methods We will work closely with the oncology team (i.e., physician, advanced practitioner, nurse) to identify patients who will soon begin or have begun treatment with chemotherapy. With permission from the oncology team, we will screen for eligible patients from outpatient clinic schedules and the inpatient malignant hematology service list. We will contact the oncology team and inform them that a patient may be eligible for the study. The oncology team will confirm whether the patient is a study candidate (including whether they are a candidate for exercise). If there is a question about eligibility, the principal investigator (PI) will be contacted and the PI will meet with the patient, review the medical records, and/or perform an assessment of eligibility if necessary. We have included options for both in-person or verbal consent to facilitate accrual ( Figure 1 ). Subject eligibility will be confirmed by both the treating physician and PI. Intervention The GO-EXCAP intervention consists of the EXCAP ©® exercise prescription, EXCAP ©® kit (i.e., activity tracker, therapeutic bands, print manual, bag), a mobile app, and a session with the exercise physiologist to deliver exercise prescription ( Figure 2 ). The intervention will last for three cycles of chemotherapy (approximately 12 weeks; up to 16 weeks accounting for treatment delay). Figure 2: Open in a new tab Geriatric-Oncology Exercise for Cancer Patients (GO-EXCAP) Mobile App The home-based exercise program, EXCAP ©® , involves two individually tailored components: a walking prescription for progressive aerobic exercise and a resistance band exercise prescription for progressive resistance training. For aerobic exercises (low to moderate intensity with rated perceived exertion of 5–8/10), 53 participants will be asked to increase the average number of daily walking steps by 5–20% weekly. 54 For resistance band exercises (low to moderate intensity with rated perceived exertion of 5–8/10), 53 participants will be provided with three color-coded therapeutic bands of varying levels of resistance and instructed to begin with one set of 8–15 repetitions of different exercises while increasing the intensity (by switching to bands with more resistance) and sets (up to four sets of 15) over the intervention period. All exercises are to be done daily. The GO-EXCAP app tracks exercise data, symptoms, and barriers to exercise adherence. Steps are synced between the app and wearable tracker automatically. Patients are able to visualize their steps on the app and compare them with other participants’ daily steps in the study. The exercise physiologist can adjust exercise prescriptions and provide strategies for patients to increase adherence via the app or over the phone. The app also has a chat and video function to facilitate communication between patients and the exercise physiologist and study team. In addition, the app includes instructional videos on the resistance band exercises. Symptom reporting and management are important components of our GO-EXCAP intervention because symptoms are common barriers to exercise. We selected common symptoms experienced by patients with myeloid neoplasms based on the Patient-Reported Outcomes version of the Common Terminology Criteria for Adverse Events. 55 We will assess symptoms at least twice weekly. For each symptom, we will use standardized severity-based algorithms and self-management strategies which were developed based on guidelines and feedback from hematology nurses ( Figure 3 ). A symptom report will be generated weekly and sent to the oncology team. Management of symptoms will be deferred to the oncology team. Figure 3: Open in a new tab Example of a standardized severity-based algorithm Study Procedures ( Figure 4 ) Figure 4: Open in a new tab Study schema At baseline, participants will complete demographic forms. They will be provided with an activity tracker (Garmin vívofit 4) to measure daily steps over 4–7 days. Study personnel will obtain cancer and treatment information from the electronic medical record (EMR). We will collect baseline measures ( Table 2 ). We will provide questionnaires via paper and pencil or electronically via Research Electronic Data Capture (REDCap) according to patients’ preferences. Participants will also provide blood samples, collected at routine blood draws, for biomarker assays. After baseline measures, participants will be randomized to either the GO-EXCAP arm or behavioral placebo control arm. Table 2: Measures Measure Description Time point Short Physical Performance Battery 12-item scale: assesses balance, gait speed, and strength; ↑ scores indicate better physical function T1, T2 Brief Fatigue Inventory 9-item scale; ↑ scores indicate worse fatigue T1, T2 Center for Epidemiologic Studies Depression Scale 20-item scale; ↑ scores indicate worse symptom T1, T2 Functional Assessment of Cancer Therapy-Leukemia 17-item scale; ↑ scores indicate better quality of life T1, T2 6 Minute Walk Test Distance (m) walked in 6 minutes: a measure of functional endurance T1, T2 Katz Activities of Daily Living 6 items; self-reported independence in 6 activities T1, T2 OARS Instrumental of Daily Living 7 items; self-reported independence in 7 activities T1, T2 Fall history Self-reported number of falls in the prior 6 months T1, T2 Nutritional status Assesses unintentional weight loss in the past 6 months and body mass index, as well as height and weight T1, T2 MOCA or MOCA-blind MOCA ranges from 0–30; assesses executive function, naming, memory, attention, language, abstraction, delayed recall, and orientation. MOCA-blind ranges from 0–22 T1, T2 OARS Physical Health Section Assesses the presence of 13 illnesses (e.g. other cancer or leukemia, arthritis, glaucoma) as well as hearing and visual impairments, and how much each problem interferes with his/her activities T1 OARS Social Support 15 items; assesses the presence of emotional and tangible support T1 Medications Number of prescribed and over-the-counter medications T1 System usability scale 10-item scale; ↑ scores indicate better usability T2 Health Information Technology Usability Evaluation Scale 20-item scale; assesses impact, perceived usefulness, perceived ease of use, and user control; ↑ scores indicate better usability T2 Activity level (step count and resistance training) Measures daily steps using a wearable device. Participant reports resistance training data on the mobile app Steps: 4–7 days prior to the initiation of intervention and 4–7 days at post-intervention. Resistance data: obtained from the app during the study duration Self-reported activity level Participant reports physical and exercise habit T1, T2 Self-Efficacy for Exercise Scale 9-item scale; measures self-efficacy for exercise; ↑ scores indicate higher selfefficacy T1, T2 Actigraphy Measures energy expenditure 4–7 days prior to the initiation of intervention and 4–7 days at postintervention Bioelectrical Impedance System Measures muscle mass; uses the RJL Bioelectrical Impedance System T1, T2 Hand Grip Strength Test Measures muscle strength; uses the Handgrip Dynamometer T1, T2 Isokinetic knee flexion/extension for lower extremities and seated shoulder diagonal for upper extremities Measures muscle strength; uses the BTS FREEEMG 1000 EMG system T1, T2 Heart rate variability Measures variation in consecutive heartbeat intervals; uses the Firstbeat® system T1, T2 Blood draw Serum levels of TNFα, sTNFR1, sTNFR2, IFNy, IL-1β, IL-2, sIL-2R, IL-6, sIL-6R, IL-8, IL-10 Genome-wide DNA methylation patterns T1, T2 Open in a new tab Abbreviations: IFNy, interferon gamma; IL-1β, interleukin-1β; IL-2, interleukin-2; IL-6, interleukin-6; IL-8, interleukin-8; IL-10, interleukin-10; MOCA, Montreal Cognitive Assessment; OARS, Older Americans Resources and Services; sIL-2R; soluble interleukin-2 receptor; sIL-6R; soluble interleukin-6 receptor; sTNFR1, soluble TNF receptor 2; sTNFR2, soluble TNF receptor 2; TNFα, tumor necrosis factor α; T1, Baseline; T2, Post-intervention Patients in the GO-EXCAP intervention arm will meet for approximately 60 minutes either in person or virtually with an American College of Sports Medicine (ACSM) certified clinical exercise physiologist who will introduce the EXCAP ©® exercise program, provide individualized walking and resistance band exercise prescriptions, demonstrate each of the resistance band exercises, and provide modifications if needed. This will be done during an in-person visit or virtually based on patients’ preference. Study personnel will help participants download the mobile app onto their electronic devices and demonstrate its features, such as how to enter exercise data into the app. If they do not have an electronic device, a tablet preloaded with the app will be provided. GO-EXCAP participants will be provided with an EXCAP ©® kit, which includes an EXCAP print manual, three therapeutic bands, and an activity tracker, to be used at home. Patients who are randomized to the behavioral placebo control arm will meet with a research physician for approximately 60 minutes in a private room or virtually via zoom/phone to review the NCI booklet Chemotherapy and You: Support for People With Cancer . The booklet includes facts about chemotherapy and its side effects. They will be provided with NCI online resources to review at home. In addition, they will be instructed to utilize the Cancer.Net (an approved patient information blog from the American Society of Clinical Oncology). The general chemotherapy education controls for time and attention with the exercise physiologist and app. While this is not the standard of care in our institution, we think that a behavioral placebo control strengthens the rigor of the pilot RCT. At post-intervention (three cycles of chemotherapy, approximately 12 weeks and up to 16 weeks accounting for cycle delay), we will collect post-intervention measures ( Table 3 ). An exit interview (approximately 30–45 min) will be conducted with participants to elicit feedback. Participants who complete both the baseline and post-intervention measures will receive $50. They may keep the EXCAP ©® kit including the activity tracker, and the behavioral placebo control group will receive the EXCAP ©® kit after completion of all study requirements (patients can meet with an exercise physiologist if they would like to). Table 3: Intervention fidelity Domain Description Study Design We will document the occurrence of all encounters between the exercise physiologist and patient. Provider Training For every five patients, we will observe one intervention delivered by the exercise physiologist. A checklist will be created to ensure all components of the intervention are delivered. We will provide training updates every 6 months to minimize drift. Intervention Delivery & Adherence The study team can monitor app usage on the app and measure the number of days the app was used and the number of days exercise data were entered into the app. Intervention Receipt After demonstration of the intervention by the exercise physiologist at baseline, we will ask participants to perform the resistance band training and enter these data into the app. Intervention Enactment At mid-intervention, we will assess steps recorded on the activity tracker and to the synced data Open in a new tab Randomization We will employ two treatment arms in a 1:1 allocation ratio: GO-EXCAP vs. behavioral placebo control. Consented subjects will be randomized with a computer-generated random numbers table with varying block sizes (e.g., 2, 4, 6). We will stratify by diagnosis (AML vs. other) and chemotherapy cycle at initiation of intervention (1–3 vs. 4 and beyond). Measures ( Table 2 ) Measures will be obtained at baseline (T1) and/or post-intervention (T2). Clinical measures We will use standardized tools to assess physical function, fatigue, mood, and QoL ( Table 1 ). 55 – 58 Primary outcome will be physical function, measured using the SPPB. 56 Secondary outcomes will be fatigue (Brief Fatigue Inventory), 57 mood (Center for Epidemiologic Studies Depression Scale), 58 and QoL (Functional Assessment of Cancer Therapy-Leukemia). 59 We will also collect other measures of interest including a geriatric assessment (e.g., social support, nutritional status, fall history, social support) ( Table 2 ). Feasibility metrics Because this is a pilot RCT, we will collect metrics such as enrollment rate (% of patients who are approached and consent), reasons for not being eligible, reasons for not consenting, drop-out rate between consent and completion of baseline assessment, reasons for drop-out, retention rate (% of patients who complete baseline assessments and subsequently complete post-intervention assessments), reasons for withdrawing from the study, mobile app usage (% of days activity tracker was worn and synced, % of days symptoms data were entered into the app, % of days resistance exercise data was entered into the app), adverse events related to the intervention, and usability assessed using the System Usability Scale ( Table 2 ). Blood Sample Collection Approximately 50 mL of fasting (if possible) blood will be drawn at baseline and post-intervention. All tubes will be collected by a study team member, processed, and then transferred to a secured −80°C freezer. Cytokines/Protein Expression Cytokines and their receptors [TNFα, soluble TNF receptor 1 (sTNFR1), soluble TNF Receptor 2 (sTNFR2), IFNy, IL-1β, interleukin-2 (IL-2), soluble interleukin-2 receptor (sIL-2R), IL-6, soluble interleukin-6 receptor (sIL-6R), interleukin-8 (IL-8), interleukin-10 (IL-10)] will be assessed via standard Luminex assays (high sensitivity assays from Millipore). We will measure samples in duplicate. DNA extraction and methylation Genomic DNA will be extracted using DNeasy Blood and Tissue kit (Qiagen) and quantified with the NanoDrop 1000 spectrophotometer. Genomic DNA (1000 ng) will be subjected to bisulfite conversion. DNA methylation microarray assay will be performed using the Illumina Infinium® Methylation EPIC Array platform, an oligonucleotide array that interrogates >850,000 CpG dinucleotides per sample. The raw data will be processed by the R package “minfi”, 60 and converted to methylation ß-value, ranging from 0 to 1, with 0 being unmethylated and 1 being fully methylated, to represent the methylation level of each CpG site. Potential residue batch effects will be inferred from the data using a Surrogate Variable Analysis (SVA), 61 and the ComBat algorithm will be used for correction. Rigorous quality-control criteria will be used for filtering at both the locus and sample levels. Intervention Quality and Fidelity To ensure quality, the GO-EXCAP intervention will be delivered by an ACSM-certified clinical exercise physiologist. Fidelity will be assessed based on the recommendations of the Treatment Fidelity Workgroup of the National Institutes of Health Behavior Change Consortium ( Table 3 ). 62 Fidelity involves the use of methodological strategies to enhance reliability and validity of a behavioral intervention. The Consortium recommends strategies in the domains of study design, provider training, treatment delivery, treatment receipt, and enactment of treatment skills. Contamination in the behavioral placebo arm While the GO-EXCAP app is available to be downloaded on the app store, participants cannot access the app (an active component of our intervention) without approval from the study team. Patients may participate in a different exercise program or regimen, or be referred to physical therapy by their healthcare team. We do not think it is ethical to ask patients not to participate in such program or physical therapy. Participants in the behavioral placebo arm will be asked to record the frequency, duration, and perceived rated exertion if they perform any resistance exercises during the study. Quality control and missing data All data will be entered into REDCap. We will visually inspect the data. If distributional assumptions are not met, we will use transformations or non-parametric methods (e.g., log transform TNFα levels). We will assess outliers to determine if they are erroneous. If they are valid, we will use sensitivity analyses (with and without outliers). Missing data are expected given our study population. The reasons for missing data will be evaluated, and the magnitude of missingness will be assessed. For biomarkers, we will use imputation methods developed specifically for data with missing values due to the detection limit. 63 , 64 Sample size Based on our initial power analysis, we estimate an accrual of 100 patients (40 per arm accounting for 20% attrition). Clinically significant and meaningful change for SPPB in older adults ranges from 0.5–1.5. 65 , 66 An SPPB change of 0.5–1.5 points translates to effect sizes of 0.25–0.75, assuming a standard deviation of 2 points. 65 , 66 Forty patients per arm, power=0.80, at α=0.10 (2-tailed), 67 will allow detection of an effect size of 0.45, which is considered clinically meaningful using analysis of covariance (ANCOVA) design 68 and assumed pre-post correlation of r=0.60. If our pre-post correlation increased to 0.7, the detectable effect size is 0.40. The estimates of standard deviation and pre-post correlation were based on our data and previous studies. 65 , 66 Statistical Analyses The primary analysis will assess the effect of the intervention on SPPB at post-intervention (T2). We will apply ANCOVA with SPPB at T2 as the outcome, and Study Arm as a factor of interest and SPPB at baseline (T1) as a covariate. To test if the effect of the intervention differs by baseline levels of SPPB (i.e., moderation), we will evaluate the Study Arm*SPPB at T1 interaction. In addition, we will evaluate the effects of other variables [e.g., age, sex, race, education, body mass index, type of myeloid neoplasms (AML vs. other), and treatment (hypomethylating agent-based vs. other)] on the relationship between study arm with SPPB at T2. We will first evaluate each individual variable as a potential predictor of SPPB and as a potential moderator of the treatment effect. We will then construct a more complex multivariate regression model. For secondary aims and other measures, we will use the same analysis plan as for SPPB. For biomarkers, descriptive statistics will be used to describe cytokine levels and methylation status at baseline and post-intervention. To explore the effect of the intervention on biomarkers, we will use the same analysis plan as for SPPB. We will explore changes in biomarkers and their relationships with clinical measures and activity levels. Results from these analyses will be applied in forming path analysis models to evaluate biomarkers as mediators of exercise-induced changes in clinical measures. 69 – 71 Statistical analysis will be conducted using SAS, R and/or MPlus. 72 , 73 Hypothesis testing will use α =0.10 (2-tailed). 74 Qualitative analyses Interviews will be transcribed and deductive thematic analyses will be used. Two independent coders trained by the PI will analyze the data. We will use a prior coding scheme developed from our single-arm pilot study and the scheme will be revised using the initial transcripts. The PI will resolve any discrepancies between the coders. Data will be used to optimize the study design, procedures, and intervention Data monitoring The URMC/WCI Data Safety Monitoring Committee (DSMC) will serve as the DSMC of record for this study. The DSMC comprises individuals with expertise spanning medicine, cancer, and biostatistics. The DSMC will provide oversight of study progress and safety by review of accrual and events once a year. The research team will submit annual progress reports of these data to the DSMC for review. The review will include the number of subjects enrolled, withdrawals, significant toxicities as described in the protocol, serious adverse events both expected and unexpected. The research team will submit a copy of the adverse event (AE) spreadsheet along with the Progress Report to the DSMC for review. Discussion To the authors’ knowledge, this is the first study that assesses a mHealth exercise intervention in older adults with myeloid neoplasms. Several pilot studies have assessed an exercise intervention for older patients with AML receiving intensive induction chemotherapy, 15 , 16 who are generally fitter than those receiving outpatient chemotherapy. Our study is one of the few that is designed for older adults with AML and other myeloid neoplasms receiving outpatient chemotherapy. Our population is highly vulnerable and at high risk of experiencing physical function decline, fatigue, mood disturbances, and worsening QoL. Our mHealth exercise intervention has been adapted and refined based on feedback from this population collected in a previously completed qualitative and single arm pilot study, 75 and it is designed specifically to meet their needs (e.g., symptoms, low exercise self-efficacy). In addition, our study focuses on genome-wide DNA methylation changes and inflammatory markers which could help us understand the mechanisms of exercise-induced benefits and guide future tailoring of clinical exercise prescriptions for older patients with cancer. Our study is novel and innovative because of the understudied vulnerable population included, use of mHealth in combination with an evidence-based behavioral intervention, and the incorporation of genome-wide DNA methylation changes in a rigorously designed pilot RCT. Several limitations should be noted. Many older patients with myeloid neoplasms require treatment delays and discontinuation due to treatment-related toxicities. In addition, some patients may stop chemotherapy and proceed to stem cell transplantation (SCT). Therefore, it is difficult to recruit a homogeneous population. Rather, we chose to include a more generalized population. We selected three cycles of chemotherapy or approximately 12 weeks and up to 16 weeks to account for treatment delays. Those who proceed to SCT prior to 12 weeks will complete post-intervention measures prior to this (i.e., the intervention duration will be shorter), because SCT is administered in the inpatient setting. Allowing such flexibility will help improve accrual. Attrition and missing data are expected; approximately 10–30% of older patients die from their disease or its treatment early in the course. 76 , 77 We have accounted for this in our sample size calculation. This is a single-center study, but we plan to recruit patients from community practices affiliated with URMC/WCI. In future trials, we will be able to expand to other institutions nationwide. In conclusion, findings from our study will inform the power calculation of a definitive trial assessing a mHealth exercise intervention compared to a behavioral placebo control on physical function and other outcomes in older patients with myeloid neoplasms receiving outpatient chemotherapy. In addition, it will allow us to refine or consider further changes in the study design, procedures, measures, intervention, and analyses. While we focus on older patients with myeloid neoplasms, this population also serves as an ideal model for studying an individually tailored mHealth exercise intervention to prevent physical function decline and improve symptoms among vulnerable older patients receiving prolonged cancer treatments. This work may also lay the foundation for the use of biomarkers in the design of behavioral interventions to improve outcomes in older patients with cancer. Acknowledgements: We wish to acknowledge Dr. Susan Rosenthal, MD, for her editorial assistance. Financial Support: The work is supported by the National Cancer Institute at the National Institute of Health (UG1 CA189961; R00CA237744 to KPL), the National Institute of Aging at the National Institute of Health (K24 AG056589 to SGM), the National Institute of Nursing Research at NIH (K24NR018621 to RS), and the Wilmot Research Fellowship Award (to KPL). We would like to acknowledge those who provided feedback as part of the Cancer and Aging Research Group (R33AG059206) and Transdisciplinary Research in Energetics and Cancer Research Education Program (TREC Training Workshop; R25CA203650).The content is solely the responsibility of the authors and does not necessarily represent the official views of the National Institutes of Health. Footnotes Publisher''s Disclaimer: This is a PDF file of an unedited manuscript that has been accepted for publication. As a service to our customers we are providing this early version of the manuscript. The manuscript will undergo copyediting, typesetting, and review of the resulting proof before it is published in its final form. Please note that during the production process errors may be discovered which could affect the content, and all legal disclaimers that apply to the journal pertain. Conflict of Interest: Dr. Loh has served as a consultant to Pfizer and Seattle Genetics, and has received honoraria from Pfizer. All other authors have no relevant conflicts of interest to report. References 1. Arber DA, Orazi A, Hasserjian R, et al. The 2016 revision to the World Health Organization classification of myeloid neoplasms and acute leukemia. Blood. 2016 2016;127(20):2391–2405. doi: 10.1182