INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(295,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12375222','Assessing the Reporting Quality of Machine Learning Algorithms in Head and Neck Oncology.','Alapati R, Renslo B, Wagoner SF, Karadaghy O, Serpedin A, Kim YE, Feucht M, Wang N, Ramesh U, Bon Nieves A, Lawrence A, Virgen C, Sawaf T, Rameau A, Bur AM.','2024','ocebm hypopharyngeal hypopharyngeal hypopharyngeal hypopharyngeal hypopharyngeal ocemb deidentification completecase recalibration algorithim offsite transparently bolsters obstructs unveil nihms2102972 supplemental_1 nihms2102972 supplemental_2 123kb nihms2102972 supplemental_3 nihms2102972 supplemental_4 naomi mlis ahip rahul alapati renslo wagoner karadaghy aisha serpedin eun feucht naomi bon nieves amelia celina virgen tuleen sawaf anais rameau bur halicek hyperspectral jbo rosado lequerica villallain lasheras eswa babier boutilier mcniven tcy mullainathan obermeyer aer p20171084 cabitza rasoini gensini kgm dhiman andaur probast e048008 adelstein oloew cebm alabi elmusrati sawazaki calone s00428 alabi elmusrati sawazaki calone ariji nozawa s11282 ariji kise oooo ariji kise kuwada ariji ariji sugita nagao s11282 bohnenberger kaderali emmm brekel mwm gouw zar bur holcomb carnielli dohopolski sher evidential ab083a s00784 costantino sampieri pirola virgilio tors csury csury balk polsby popper histomorphological dohopolski sher aleatoric abb71c whitley jedani ftir aperture snom d1an00922b farrokhian holcomb dimon e227226 fazelpour vejdani jahromi kaliaev forghani reinhold fouad landini mehanna hybridisation crafted compmedimag fujima andreu arasa meibom zeevi isaiah kochanny koshy spiotto e2025881 mercaldo santone fabnet jbhi s40644 afzal arden artmed jurmeister bockmayr seegerer aaw8513 kaddi jbhi aneja loganadane payabvash likitlersuang bontempi e360 e369 karadaghy shew bur kawamura multilayer perceptron subnetwork quaas quantius eun greca bogowicz konukoglu compbiomed leitheiser capper seegerer marschner mermod jourdan cintra e190039 rycan nauta klausch karadaghy fassas arambula bur invasions hrmas s11306 ranjbar zwart romeo cuocolo anticanres joms mribased shew karadaghy e851 e856 zwanenburg stepp farquhar sheth oncoimmune tighe fabris bjoms yamashiro heianna s12957 khurram rajpoot modpat duggar weusthof burkart semmelmayer ijms24108938 gensheimer kernelized compmedimag kyp guillaud poh e0259529 ctbased folkert ab8956 jcc khondker lajkosz e2335377 sounderajah ashrafian golub e047709 norgeot quer rashidi modave tighe fdgth daneshjou rotemberg jayakumar sounderajah normahani euaff kors rijnbeek teno e230397 jamahealthforum panch mattie atun jogh podgurski lasko siew matheny ocx030 artint keiser jopart muaa019 giffen herhausen fahse perils jbusres kubilius i004ir nihms2102972 supplemental_1 nihms2102972 supplemental_2 123kb nihms2102972 supplemental_3 nihms2102972 supplemental_4','Abstract Objective: This study aimed to assess reporting quality of machine learning (ML) algorithms in the head and neck oncology literature using the TRIPOD-AI criteria. Data Sources: A comprehensive search was conducted using PubMed, Scopus, Embase, and Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews, incorporating search terms related to “artificial intelligence,” “machine learning,” “deep learning,” “neural network,” and various head and neck neoplasms. Review Methods: Two independent reviewers analyzed each published study for adherence to the 65-point TRIPOD-AI criteria. Items were classified as “Yes,” “No,” or “NA” for each publication. The proportion of studies satisfying each TRIPOD-AI criterion was calculated. Additionally, the evidence level for each study was evaluated independently by two reviewers using the Oxford Centre for Evidence-Based Medicine (OCEBM) Levels of Evidence. Discrepancies were reconciled through discussion until consensus was reached. Results: The study highlights the need for improvements in ML algorithm reporting in head and neck oncology. This includes more comprehensive descriptions of datasets, standardization of model performance reporting, and increased sharing of ML models, data, and code with the research community. Adoption of TRIPOD-AI is necessary for achieving standardized ML research reporting in head and neck oncology. Conclusion: Current reporting of ML algorithms hinders clinical application, reproducibility, and understanding of the data used for model training. To overcome these limitations and improve patient and clinician trust, ML developers should provide open access to models, code, and source data, fostering iterative progress through community critique, thus enhancing model accuracy and mitigating biases. Keywords: artificial intelligence, head and neck oncology, machine learning, TRIPOD-AI INTRODUCTION Machine learning (ML) is a sub-field of artificial intelligence (AI) that focuses on training algorithms to recognize patterns in existing data, enabling them to make predictions when provided with new data. ML has been increasingly investigated as a head and neck oncology care decision-making tool, as it has shown promise in predicting cancer diagnosis and prognosis, developing adaptive clinical trial designs, rapidly identifying radiographic anomalies, delineating surgical anatomy, and identifying malignancies. 1 - 3 Despite these advancements, the clinical integration of ML algorithms in otolaryngology is limited. Clinician skepticism, coupled with ethical, legal, and dataset bias concerns, hampers the adoption of these models. 4 , 5 To navigate these challenges, it is important to establish a standard of transparency and rigor in the reporting of ML research. Without it, the potential benefits of ML cannot be reliably implemented into clinical practice. Acknowledging this need, the Transparent Reporting of a multivariable prediction model for Individual Prognosis or Diagnosis (TRIPOD) criteria were created. Comprising 22 items, the TRIPOD criteria provide a structured approach to report multivariable prediction model studies. These criteria encompass study design, participant characteristics, variables used in the model, details of the model development and validation process, and performance metrics. 6 The extension of these guidelines into the TRIPOD-AI criteria aims to improve the transparency, reproducibility, and quality of ML studies, facilitating the evaluation of ML studies’ validity and applicability. 7 With the increasing number of ML algorithms published in the head and neck oncology literature, there has yet to be any standardized assessment of the quality of reporting among these studies. This study presents a systematic appraisal of the transparency and reporting quality of algorithms published in the head and neck oncology literature using the TRIPOD-AI criteria. The findings serve to identify reporting gaps and opportunities for improvement to pave the way for clinical integration of reliable ML tools. METHODS Data Sources and Searches In collaboration with a health science librarian, we searched the following databases from January 1, 2017, through the date of the search on June 17, 2024: PubMed, Scopus, Embase, and Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews. The following search criteria were utilized: (“artificial intelligence” OR “machine learning” OR “deep learning” OR “Neural Network”) AND (“head and neck neoplasms” OR “Squamous cell carcinoma of head and neck” OR “laryngeal neoplasms” OR “mouth neoplasms” OR “hypopharyngeal neoplasms” OR “oropharyngeal neoplasms” OR “pharyngeal neoplasms”) ( Table I ). TABLE I. Search Criteria. PubMed (“artificial intelligence” OR “machine learning” OR “deep learning” OR “Neural Network”) AND (“head and neck neoplasms” OR “Squamous cell carcinoma of head and neck” OR “laryngeal neoplasms” OR “mouth neoplasms” OR “hypopharyngeal neoplasms” OR “oropharyngeal neoplasms” OR “pharyngeal neoplasms”) 486 Embase ((“artificial intelligence” OR “machine learning” OR “deep learning” OR “Neural Network”) AND (“head and neck neoplasms” OR “Squamous cell carcinoma of head and neck” OR “laryngeal neoplasms” OR “mouth neoplasms” OR “hypopharyngeal neoplasms” OR “oropharyngeal neoplasms” OR “pharyngeal neoplasms”) 20 Scopus (“artificial intelligence” OR “machine learning” OR “deep learning” OR “Neural Network”) AND (“head and neck neoplasms” OR “Squamous cell carcinoma of head and neck” OR “laryngeal neoplasms” OR “mouth neoplasms” OR “hypopharyngeal neoplasms” OR “oropharyngeal neoplasms” OR “pharyngeal neoplasms”) 333 Cochrane (“artificial intelligence” OR “machine learning” OR “deep learning” OR “Neural Network”) AND (“head and neck neoplasms” OR “Squamous cell carcinoma of head and neck” OR “laryngeal neoplasms” OR “mouth neoplasms” OR “hypopharyngeal neoplasms” OR “oropharyngeal neoplasms” OR “pharyngeal neoplasms”) 5 Total 844 Open in a new tab The search criteria for each database and the respective number of studies found. Study Selection All studies that developed, utilized, or integrated an ML algorithm for treatment decision making for patients with head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) were included in this review. This included any studies that would assist in choosing a certain treatment path based on the National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) head and neck guidelines. 8 Studies were excluded if they were non-primary articles, did not help determine a treatment pathway in accordance with the NCCN guidelines for primary treatment, were not tailored to HNSCC, did not have full text available, or were not published in English. Data Extraction Titles and abstracts were independently screened by three reviewers. Studies meeting inclusion criteria were further screened with full-text review for final inclusion. Disagreements were resolved by consensus with the senior author. A flow diagram of study selection is included ( Supplemental 1 ). Each study underwent an in-depth independent review for the presence of the TRIPOD-AI criteria by at least two reviewers experienced in AI research (BR, OK, AS, YK). The TRIPOD-AI criteria consists of 65 checklist items ( Supplemental 2 ), 7 and each item was given a score of “Yes,” “No,” or “NA” as applicable to each study. Discrepancies between reviewers were discussed until consensus was reached. Data Synthesis and Analysis The level of evidence of each study was independently assessed by two reviewers using the Oxford Centre for Evidence-Based Medicine (OCEMB) Levels of Evidence. 9 Additionally, the interrater agreement was calculated for each TRIPOD-AI criterion using Cohen’s Kappa. TRIPOD-AI scores were calculated by assigning 1 point per checklist item met, for a maximum of 65 points. Because not all items were applicable to each study, criteria marked “NA” were excluded from score calculations. Furthermore, we calculated the percentage of applicable criteria met for each study. For each criterion, we calculated percentage of studies that met that criterion, excluding studies where the criterion was not applicable. RESULTS A total of 844 records were identified across all databases searched. After removing 329 duplicates, 515 studies underwent title and abstract screening. Three-hundred and thirty-one studies were excluded for the following reasons: Review/meta-analyses ( n = 41); not related to treatment decision making ( n = 128); related to tumor segmentation ( n = 73); about radiation dosing/planning ( n = 21); being out of scope ( n = 55); not having full text ( n = 11); and not being in English ( n = 2). Afterward, 184 remaining studies underwent full-text review, leading to the exclusion of 107 studies for not being a primary study ( n = 7), not related to treatment decision making ( n = 83), focused on tumor segmentation ( n = 7), related to radiation dosing/planning ( n = 6), being out of scope ( N = 2), or not being in English ( n = 2). A grand total of 77 studies utilizing ML algorithms to predict clinical outcomes in head and neck cancer patients were included in this review. 10 - 86 The average Cohen’s kappa across the measured TRIPOD-AI criteria was 0.367, representing a fair degree of consensus between the reviewers. TRIPOD-AI Scores The TRIPOD-AI scores of studies ranged from 22 to 49 out of 65, with higher scores representing greater transparency and reporting quality. When evaluated against the total applicable number of TRIPOD-AI criteria for each study, the median percentage of criteria met was 64% (IQR: 56%–74%, 18%; range: 42%–85%, Supplemental 3 ). Title, Abstract, Background, and Objectives Although all studies developed an ML prediction model, less than half identified in their study title that they utilized one ( n = 34, 44.2%). Almost all studies provided a clear medical context and rationale for developing or validating their prediction model ( n = 75, 97.4%). Most studies specified an objective (model development, validation, or both) ( n = 66, 85.7%, Table II , Supplemental 4 ). TABLE II. Reviewer TRIPOD-AI Data Characteristics Checklist. Criteria Inter-reviewer reliability Data characteristics N =77 Cohen’s K Describe the study design or source of data (e.g., randomized trial, cohort, routine care or registry data), separately for the development and validation (test) datasets, if applicable. 76 (98.7%) 0.252 Describe the origin of the data, and how data were identified, requested and collected. 75 (97.4%) Specify key elements of the study setting (e.g., primary care, secondary care, general population) including the number and location of centers or data sources. 68 (88.3%) 0.219 Open in a new tab Reviewers went through each of the selected studies with the following introduction section of TRIPOD-AI checklist and rated whether the study had mentioned the respective criteria. Inter-reviewer reliability was calculated with reporting Cohen’s K. Data Characteristics Most studies included thorough details on their design and data origin ( n = 75, 97.4%). Location and data sources were also described in almost all the studies ( n = 76, 98.7%). However, dates of participant data collection were stated in less than two-thirds of studies ( n = 50, 64.9%, Table II , Supplemental 4). Data Quality While most studies outlined data pre-processing steps, including data cleaning and quality checks ( n = 63, 81.8%), close to one-third provided sample size justification ( n = 29, 37.7%), and less than one-third discussed how missing data were handled ( n = 23, 29.9%, Table III , Supplemental 4 ). TABLE III. Reviewer TRIPOD-AI Data Characteristics Checklist. Criteria N =77 Inter-reviewer reliability Data quality Cohen’s K Describe any data pre-processing steps, including any cleaning, harmonization, sampling, linkage, deidentification methods, and quality checks. 63 (81.8%) 0.137 Explain how the study size was arrived at (and provide a justification that the study size was sufficient). Report assumptions and estimates to support any sample size calculation. 29 (37.7%) 0.466 Describe how missing data were handled (e.g., completecase analysis, single imputation, multiple imputation) with details of any imputation or other data augmentation method. 23 (29.9%) NA = 3 (3.9%) 0.618 Open in a new tab Reviewers went through each of the selected studies with the following data quality section of TRIPOD-AI checklist and rated whether the study had mentioned the respective criteria. Inter-reviewer reliability was calculated with reporting Cohen’s K. Algorithm Transparency The analysis of the algorithm transparency criteria is depicted in Table IV and Supplemental 4 . Most studies clearly defined the outcomes predicted by the algorithm ( n = 74, 96.1%), and offered definitions for all predictors and features used to create the multivariable prediction model ( n = 70, 90.9%). The source of these predictors and information on potential biases were disclosed in over two-thirds of the studies ( n = 54, 70.1%). TABLE IV. Reviewer TRIPOD-AI Algorithm Transparency Checklist. Criteria N = 77 Inter-reviewer reliability Algorithm transparency Cohen’s K Clearly define the outcome (e.g., ground truth or reference standard) that is predicted by the prediction model (including the time horizon), including how and when assessed and the rationale for choosing this outcome measurement (if alternatives exist) 74 (96.1%) 0.388 Clearly define all predictors/features used in developing the multivariable prediction model, including how and when they were measured. Consider using supplementary material for large numbers of predictors. 70 (90.9%) 0.506 Provide a detailed description of the model, including inputs, outputs, all intermediate layers, and connections. 32 (41.6%) NA = 1 (1.3%) Specify all measures used to assess model performance (e.g., discrimination, calibration) and, if relevant, to compare multiple models. 72 (93.5%) 0.232 Describe any model updating (e.g., recalibration) arising from the validation (testing), either overall or for particular populations or settings 30 (39.0%) NA = 1 (1.3%) Provide details on the full prediction model to allow predictions for individuals to allow third-party evaluation and implementation (e.g., regression coefficients, input parameters, sharing of code/any dependencies). Provide reasons for not sharing code. 17 (22.0%) Open in a new tab Reviewers went through each of the selected studies with the following algorithm transparency section of TRIPOD-AI checklist and rated whether the study had mentioned the respective criteria. Inter-reviewer reliability was calculated with reporting Cohen’s K. Detailed descriptions of the model architecture, including inputs, outputs, and intermediate connections ( n = 32, 41.6%) and updates made to the model following validation ( n = 30, 39.0%), were provided in less than half of the studies. Methods for selecting the final model were reported in most studies ( n = 67, 87.0%); however, only a small number of studies provided sufficient details on the full prediction model for third-party evaluation and implementation ( n = 17, 22.0%). Algorithm Analysis Table V and Supplemental 4 show the results of the algorithm analysis checklist. The participant flow throughout the study along with a summary of follow-up time was outlined in most studies ( n = 57, 74.0%). While over half of the studies ( n = 43, 55.8%) included performance measures to quantify whether the model’s progression through training and development, the majority failed to describe their methods for analyzing performance errors or error identification ( n = 73, 94.8%). Results from any model updating, including details of the updated model and subsequent performance, were not shared in over half of the studies ( n = 43, 55.8%). TABLE V. Reviewer TRIPOD-AI Algorithm Analysis Checklist. Criteria N = 77 Inter-reviewer reliability Algorithim analysis Cohen’s K Report the characteristics overall and where applicable for each data source or setting, including the key dates, key predictors/features (including demographics, ethnicity), treatments received, sample size, number of outcome events, follow-up time, and amount of missing data. 51 (66.2%) 0.341 Describe the results of any analysis of performance errors and how errors were identified, where applicable. If no such analysis was planned or done, explain why not. 4 (5.2%) Explain how (and when in the clinical pathway) to use the prediction AI model to permit third-party testing and implementation. 22 (28.5%) 0.272 Describe how the AI model will be integrated into the target setting and clinical pathway, including any onsite or offsite requirements (e.g., hardware/software requirements). 23 (29.9%) Describe how poor quality or unavailable input data will be assessed and handled when implementing the AI model. 3 (3.9%) Specify whether human-AI interaction will be required in the handling of the input data (e.g., steps required to go from the raw clinical data into a form usable by the model), and what level of expertise is required of users. 12 (15.6%) Open in a new tab Reviewers went through each of the selected studies with the following algorithm analysis section of TRIPOD-AI checklist and rated whether the study had mentioned the respective criteria. Inter-reviewer reliability was calculated with reporting Cohen’s K. Furthermore, information about how and when to use the AI model in a clinical pathway for third-party testing and implementation was supplied in only a few studies ( n = 22, 28.5%). Likewise, integration of the AI model into the target setting and clinical pathway was described in a small number of studies ( n = 23, 29.9%). Finally, the assessment and handling of poor quality or unavailable input data during AI model implementation were discussed in only three studies (3.9%). Interpretation and Implications The analysis of the TRIPOD-AI checklist for study interpretation and implications is displayed in Table VI . The studies’ limitations, including sample size, missing data, and biases, were addressed in most studies ( n = 70, 90.9%). Most studies also discussed the potential use of the model and its implications for future research, such as generalizability and applicability across different settings ( n = 74, 96.1%). In addition, the potential impact of the ML model and its outputs on clinical practice and work-up was clearly explained in almost all the studies ( n = 72, 93.5%). However, only a quarter of studies reported access to the ML model and/or its code ( n = 19, 24.7%). TABLE VI. Reviewer TRIPOD-AI Interpretations and Implications Checklist. Criteria N = 77 Inter-reviewer reliability Interpretations and implications Cohen’s K Discuss any limitations of the study (such as a non-representative sample, sample size, overfitting, missing data) and their effects on biases, statistical uncertainty, and generalizability. 70 (90.9%) 0.233 Discuss the potential use of the model and implications for future research, with a specific view to generalizability and applicability of the model across different settings or (sub)populations. 73 (94.8%) 0.134 Make clear how the AI/ML model and its outputs may be used and change clinical practice and work-up. 72 (93.5%) State whether and how the AI model and/or its code can be accessed, including any restrictions to access or re-use. 19 (24.7%) 0.487 Open in a new tab Reviewers went through each of the selected studies with the following interpretations and implications section of TRIPOD-AI checklist and rated whether the study had mentioned the respective criteria. Inter-reviewer reliability was calculated with reporting Cohen’s K. DISCUSSION In recent years, the growth of ML technology has resulted in a rising number of publications describing the development and validation of prediction-based models in head and neck oncology. As these models become more prevalent, they must be evaluated and reported transparently to allow for reproducibility. Multiple checklists have been developed to enhance the reporting standards of ML models. APPRAISE-AI, which has been validated against an expert consensus and the TRIPOD checklist, is designed to quantify the overall quality of clinical AI research, while STARD-AI is tailored to assess the diagnostic accuracy of AI applications. 87 , 88 Similarly, the MI-CLAIM checklist aims to enhance transparency by detailing the minimum information necessary for understanding a model’s health care application. 89 Despite the strengths of these tools, TRIPOD-AI is distinct in its appraisal of both prognostic and diagnostic multivariable prediction models. This is pertinent in head and neck oncology, where the predictive utility of ML models holds substantial clinical value, assisting in prognostication and informing therapeutic decision making. Additionally, the TRIPOD-AI criteria aim to facilitate transparency of ML models by promoting clear reporting of data quality, algorithm development, and analysis. By adhering to these guidelines, researchers can address the strengths and weaknesses of their models, accurately compare models, and contribute to the future development of reliable prediction models. 7 Furthermore, adherence to these guidelines fosters a collaborative research culture, encouraging sharing of model specifications, code, and data pre-processing details. This transparency bolsters reproducibility and independent validation, ultimately enhancing the quality and generalizability of ML prediction models in head and neck oncology. 7 , 90 This review assessed the reporting quality of 61 ML-based multivariable prediction model studies in head and neck oncology using the TRIPOD-AI checklist as a bench-mark. Our findings revealed an overall poor adherence with the median percentage of criteria met being 65%, underscoring the need for more uniform reporting of ML model development and validation if these models are to be used in a clinical setting. The advancement of ML models in health care research relies on several key practices related to the TRIPOD-AI criteria for model quality. First, data cleaning and pre-processing, which ensure data quality, are pivotal for model performance, bias reduction, and training efficiency. 91 - 94 Poor data quality, exemplified by the “garbage in, garbage out” principle, which states that low-quality training data will yield poor predictions, can lead to flawed decisions and potential bias propagation in clinical contexts. 93 , 95 - 97 Likewise, to optimize model generalizability, data collection must encompass diverse sources, representative of the target population. 97 , 98 Several studies in the current head and neck oncology ML literature fail to consistently report their processes for data cleansing and diverse sampling. This lack of transparency hinders clinical application and reproducibility, as it limits the ability of physicians and researchers to understand and evaluate the data employed for model training. Transparency in the reporting of model algorithms and analysis is also essential for translating their use into clinical practice. Insufficient transparency in ML research can undermine user trust and impede model refinement. 99 , 100 Poor reporting quality hinders the identification of potential issues and obstructs reproducibility, preventing iterative progress. 92 , 101 Some studies reviewed may perpetuate physician mistrust in ML due to unclear reporting of the model development and refinement process. To combat this mistrust, ML developers in otolaryngology should advocate for open access to ML models and their code, potentially via medical-focused repositories similar to GitHub or the Open Science Framework (OSF). 102 This would allow other developers and physicians to readily understand and critique models for improvement, potentially reducing apprehension. Furthermore, the consistent feedback from publicly available code prompts researchers to routinely update models with new data to counteract “model drift,” a phenomenon where the model becomes inaccurate secondary to environmental changes, such as novel diseases or treatments. 98 This ongoing process would not only ensure model relevance but also unveil and mitigate unseen biases, further refining the models’ accuracy. 96 , 97 This study has several limitations. First, this review only included studies in head and neck oncology, so the results may not be generalizable to other areas within otolaryngology or medicine. Additionally, many of the TRIPOD-AI criteria are inherently subjective. Although this review had a fair level of interrater reliability and attempted to mitigate differences in interpretation by employing multiple reviewers, variation in interpretation is still possible. For instance, independent reviewers had poor interrater reliability when assessing whether studies had accurately described aspects of data characteristics (describing source data and study settings), data quality (data pre-processing steps), and algorithm transparency (specifying model building procedures, measures used to assess model performance, model updates, and model output), suggesting these aspects of the TRIPOD-AI criteria may be more subjective. This may be an opportunity to improve these criteria, as they could be revised to provide clearer definitions or detailed examples to reduce the subjectivity and improve consistency when reporting and appraising ML models. Furthermore, different journals may have differing requirements for manuscript preparation, which could affect reporting of the TRIPOD-AI criteria; however, this study did not evaluate publication requirements. Similarly, the journal and its target audience might impact adherence to standardized reporting guidelines. For example, a more technical journal would require in-depth methodological explanations, possibly resulting in a higher TRIPOD-AI score. Conversely, a more clinically oriented journal might not require such stringent adherence to reporting guidelines, resulting in a lower TRIPOD-AI score. However, the purpose of TRIPOD-AI is to ensure minimum reporting standards of ML models which should not vary based on a journal’s audience. Sufficient details of the methods should be provided to enable a reader to replicate and validate the study’s findings. If technical details are not suitable for a particular journal’s audience, they should nonetheless be included in the supplemental materials . Future studies could build on the current study by applying the TRIPOD-AI criteria to other studies within otolaryngology to assess reporting quality or by evaluating study reporting in the context of publication requirements to provide suggestions for more uniform reporting across journals. CONCLUSION AND IMPLICATIONS This appraisal highlights the current level of quality and transparency in machine learning model reporting within head and neck oncology and identifies key areas for improvement. Specifically, ML developers must increase their reporting in the TRIPOD-AI domains relating to data processing, algorithm transparency and analysis, and open access to these models. Uniform reporting, potentially by utilizing the TRIPOD-AI guidelines, is essential to ensuring the validity and reproducibility of results and will help improve physician acceptance of ML in practice. Future ML studies in the head and neck oncology field should adhere to the TRIPOD-AI guidelines to disseminate advances in this field and ultimately improve clinical decision-making and patient outcomes. Supplementary Material Supplemental 1 NIHMS2102972-supplement-Supplemental_1.pdf (74.8KB, pdf) Supplemental 2 NIHMS2102972-supplement-Supplemental_2.pdf (123KB, pdf) Supplemental 3 NIHMS2102972-supplement-Supplemental_3.pdf (227.9KB, pdf) Supplemental 4 NIHMS2102972-supplement-Supplemental_4.docx (33.1KB, docx) Additional supporting information may be found in the online version of this article. ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS We would like to thank Naomi Bishop, MLIS, AHIP for helping with creating and running the search criteria across the different databases listed above. Footnotes Presented as a poster at the American Head and Neck Society 11th International Conference, Montreal, QC, July 10, 2023. The authors have no funding, financial relationships, or conflicts of interest to disclose. Contributor Information Rahul Alapati, Department of Otolaryngology-Head & Neck Surgery, University of Kansas Medical Center, Kansas City, Kansas, U.S.A.. Bryan Renslo, Department of Otolaryngology-Head & Neck Surgery, Thomas Jefferson University, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, U.S.A.. Sarah F. Wagoner, Department of Otolaryngology-Head & Neck Surgery, University of Kansas Medical Center, Kansas City, Kansas, U.S.A.. Omar Karadaghy, Department of Otolaryngology-Head & Neck Surgery, University of Kansas Medical Center, Kansas City, Kansas, U.S.A.. Aisha Serpedin, Department of Otolaryngology-Head & Neck Surgery, Weill Cornell, New York City, New York, U.S.A.. Yeo Eun Kim, Department of Otolaryngology-Head & Neck Surgery, Weill Cornell, New York City, New York, U.S.A.